  $skt$skin-of-colour-society-global-council-south-africa$skt$,
  $skt$South African Dermatology Just Joined a Global Push to Fix Skin-of-Colour Care$skt$,
  $skt$The Skin of Color Society's new Global Council includes South Africa as a founding member, aiming to close a real research gap in skin-of-colour dermatology.$skt$,
  $skb$Yes: South African dermatology now has a seat at the table of a new global initiative specifically built to close the skin-of-colour research gap, with the Dermatological Society of South Africa named as a founding member of the newly announced SOCS Global Council.

## Quick answer
- The Skin of Color Society (SOCS), the world's leading professional dermatology organisation focused on skin-of-colour care, announced its Global Council on 1 September 2026.
- The Council unites leaders from 22 international dermatology organisations, including the Dermatological Society of South Africa as a founding member.
- The goal is shared clinical guidelines, coordinated research, regulatory guidance and public education, not a single blanket fix for all skin-of-colour concerns.
- Skincare research has historically skewed toward lighter skin types, leaving gaps in treatments for conditions like melasma, keloid scarring and certain forms of hair loss.
- South Africa's genuinely diverse population and existing skin-of-colour researchers make it a valuable, practical site for this kind of coordinated research.

## What actually got announced
Skincare research has a data problem, and it is one most brands would rather you did not think about too hard. For decades, a huge share of the clinical research behind common skincare and dermatology treatments was conducted primarily on lighter skin types. Not maliciously, mostly, but structurally: research funding, major dermatology institutions and clinical trial populations have historically skewed toward populations in Europe and North America. The result is a gap that shows up in real ways. Some treatments behave differently on melanin-rich skin, some conditions present differently and get missed or misdiagnosed, and a lot of general skincare advice turns out to be less general than it sounds once you actually check who it was tested on.

That gap is exactly what a new international initiative announced this week is trying to close, and South Africa is one of the founding participants. The Skin of Color Society (SOCS), the world's leading professional dermatology organisation focused specifically on skin-of-colour care, announced the launch of its SOCS Global Council on 1 September 2026: a strategic alliance bringing together leaders from 22 international dermatology organisations to work on this problem together.

The Dermatological Society of South Africa is one of the named founding members, alongside dermatology bodies from Sudan, Ethiopia, Egypt (two separate organisations), Madagascar and Tanzania, plus additional groups from across Asia, the Caribbean, Mexico and Oceania. The Council is co-chaired by Dr Zakiya Pressley Rice in Atlanta and Dr Michelle Rodrigues in Melbourne, and according to SOCS, it had actually been quietly working since its initial launch in February 2025 before this week's public announcement widened the coalition. Current SOCS President Dr Rebecca Vasquez described the goal as inviting the international dermatology community to join, ensuring diverse perspectives are represented across the organisations involved.

## Why this isn't just a nice gesture
Here is the thing about skin of colour as a category: it is not one thing. It spans a huge range of melanin levels, undertones and skin behaviours across South Africa's own population alone, let alone globally. Treating it as a single monolithic research gap to solve would be its own kind of oversimplification.

What the Council says it is actually aiming for is more specific and more useful: shared clinical guidelines, coordinated research collaboration, regulatory guidance, and public education initiatives, built by pooling expertise across dermatology bodies that have each been working on pieces of this problem somewhat in isolation until now. That coordination matters because conditions like keloid scarring, melasma, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation and certain forms of hair loss and alopecia genuinely do show up differently, and sometimes more severely, in melanin-rich skin, and treatment protocols developed without that population in the original research do not always translate cleanly.

South Africa is not a passive participant in this space, either. The Dermatological Society of South Africa already has genuinely well-regarded researchers working specifically on skin-of-colour dermatology. Dr Ncoza Dlova, Head of Dermatology at the University of KwaZulu-Natal in Durban, has built a career focused on skin-of-colour conditions and was the first Black African dean at UKZN. Professor Nonhlanhla Khumalo, who trained through the College of Dermatologists of South Africa and holds a PhD from the University of Cape Town, is recognised as one of the first Black dermatology specialists in the country and has contributed research specifically on hair and pigmentary disorders relevant to South African patients. Their work is part of exactly the kind of local expertise this global coalition is trying to connect and amplify, rather than starting from scratch.

## The sunscreen example everyone in South Africa has actually felt
You do not need a clinical trial to have noticed one very ordinary version of this research gap: sunscreen that leaves a visible white or grey cast on deeper skin tones. That is not an accident of chemistry that nobody could have predicted. It is a well-documented consequence of formulas being tested and optimised primarily on lighter skin during development, so the invisible finish a brand promises often was not actually checked against the full range of skin tones it would end up being sold to.

It sounds like a cosmetic annoyance more than a health issue, until you remember what it actually does in practice: SPF is the single most effective daily habit for preventing further pigmentation and skin damage, and South Africa's UV index is genuinely among the highest in the world. If a sunscreen leaves a cast that makes someone stop using it, or only apply a thin, under-protective layer to avoid the grey tinge, the formulation gap has turned into a real gap in sun protection. This is a small, concrete example of exactly the kind of thing a properly resourced, globally coordinated skin-of-colour research effort is meant to catch before it reaches your bathroom shelf, rather than leaving individual brands to eventually notice the complaints on their own.

## What this could actually mean for South African patients
It is easy for an announcement like this to read as abstract, dermatology societies coordinating internationally sounds like it belongs in a trade newsletter, not something that shows up in your bathroom cabinet. But the practical downstream effects, if the Council delivers on its stated goals, are genuinely relevant: clearer, evidence-based guidance on treating hyperpigmentation and post-inflammatory marks specifically for melanin-rich skin rather than advice extrapolated from research on lighter skin types; better-informed treatment protocols for keloid scarring, which disproportionately affects patients with more melanin and remains under-researched relative to how common it is; more accurate diagnostic guidance for conditions that present differently on darker skin, since eczema, psoriasis and certain skin cancers can look meaningfully different and get under-diagnosed or diagnosed later as a result; and a stronger evidence base for haircare and scalp conditions like traction alopecia and central centrifugal cicatricial alopecia, which are more common in patients of African descent and have historically had thinner research support.

None of that happens overnight from one announcement. International coalitions like this move at the pace of research funding, publication cycles and consensus-building, which is realistically a matter of years, not months. It is worth being honest about that instead of overselling a press release as an instant fix.

## Key facts
- The SOCS Global Council was publicly announced on 1 September 2026, though it had been working quietly since February 2025.
- It unites 22 international dermatology organisations, including founding member the Dermatological Society of South Africa.
- Other African founding members include dermatology bodies from Sudan, Ethiopia, Egypt and Madagascar, plus Tanzania.
- The Council is co-chaired by Dr Zakiya Pressley Rice (Atlanta) and Dr Michelle Rodrigues (Melbourne).
- South African researchers already working in this space include Dr Ncoza Dlova (UKZN) and Professor Nonhlanhla Khumalo (UCT-trained).

## What this means for your skin
If you have keloid scarring, stubborn post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, melasma, or hair thinning around the hairline, this is a signal that the evidence base behind future treatment guidance for exactly these conditions is getting more coordinated and more resourced globally, with South African expertise directly involved rather than waiting for guidance developed elsewhere to eventually filter down. It does not change what works today, but it points toward better-validated options over the next few years.

## Who should pay attention
This is most relevant if you have ever been told a skincare or dermatology recommendation is one size fits all and suspected it was not developed with skin like yours in mind, or if you are actively managing keloid scarring, melasma, post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, or hair and scalp conditions like traction alopecia or CCCA. It also matters for anyone choosing sunscreen and dealing with the visible-cast problem on deeper skin tones.

## Ingredients and products to know
- Sunscreen formulations: watch for genuine tint-matching and no-white-cast claims that are actually tested across a range of skin tones, not just marketed that way.
- Tyrosinase-inhibiting actives (azelaic acid, alpha arbutin, tranexamic acid): commonly used for pigmentary conditions more prevalent in melanin-rich skin.
- Silicone-free, scalp-friendly formulations: relevant to ongoing research on traction alopecia and CCCA.
- Keloid-specific treatments: pressure therapy, silicone sheeting and steroid injections remain the current evidence-based options pending further research.

## The South African context
Few countries have a population with as much genuine diversity in skin tone, skin type and hair texture living under one health system and one regulatory authority. That is a real, practical advantage for the kind of research this coalition says it wants to run: rather than trying to generalise from a narrow population and hope it translates, South African researchers and patients are positioned to contribute data that is directly useful across a huge range of skin presentations, from very fair to very deeply pigmented, often within the same extended family. Combined with the country's climate extremes and correspondingly serious UV exposure, it is close to an ideal environment for testing whether new guidance actually holds up outside a lab.

## Frequently asked questions
- What is the Skin of Color Society? SOCS is the world's leading professional dermatology organisation focused specifically on skin-of-colour care, based in the United States.
- Which South African body joined the Global Council? The Dermatological Society of South Africa is a named founding member.
- Will this change my skincare routine right away? Not immediately. Coordinated research and updated clinical guidelines typically take years to develop and filter into product formulation and treatment protocols.
- Why does skin-of-colour-specific research matter? Because conditions like melasma, keloid scarring and certain forms of hair loss present differently, and sometimes more severely, in melanin-rich skin, and generic research does not always translate.

## What to do this week
1. If you are dealing with keloid scarring, stubborn hyperpigmentation or hair thinning, seek out a dermatologist with specific experience treating skin of colour rather than defaulting to generic advice.
2. When choosing a sunscreen, test it on your actual skin tone in daylight before committing, rather than trusting an invisible-finish claim on the label.
3. Keep an eye on updated guidance from the Dermatological Society of South Africa over the coming year as this coalition's work progresses.
4. If a piece of skincare advice is described as universal, consider whether it was actually developed with skin like yours in mind.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is not paid or sponsored content. Information here is based on the Skin of Color Society's public announcement and publicly available professional biographies of the named South African dermatologists. It is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about pigmentation, scarring or hair loss concerns specific to your skin.$skb$,
