  $skt$hormonal-acne-flare-ups$skt$,
  $skt$What Actually Causes Hormonal Acne Flare-Ups (And What Helps)$skt$,
  $skt$Hormonal acne clusters on the jawline, tracks your cycle, and needs a different approach than surface breakouts.$skt$,
  $skb$Hormonal acne flares because monthly shifts in oestrogen, progesterone and androgens change how much oil your skin makes and how easily your pores clog, and it is most recognisable as deep, tender breakouts along the jawline and chin that arrive on a roughly predictable cycle.

## Quick answer

- Hormonal acne is driven by androgen sensitivity in oil glands, not poor hygiene — washing your face more will not fix it.
- It typically clusters along the jaw, chin and lower cheeks and shows up as deep, tender bumps rather than surface whiteheads.
- It commonly flares in the week before a period, during perimenopause, after stopping hormonal contraception, or with conditions like PCOS.
- Topical actives (retinoids, salicylic acid, benzoyl peroxide) help, but genuinely hormonal patterns often need a dermatologist for options like spironolactone or combined oral contraceptives.
- Consistency over months, not a single product, is what actually changes the pattern.

## What is actually happening in your skin

Every oil gland (sebaceous gland) in your skin has receptors for androgens — testosterone and its more potent relative, dihydrotestosterone (DHT). When androgen activity rises, or when your gland is simply more sensitive to normal androgen levels, it produces more sebum. That thicker, more abundant oil mixes with dead skin cells inside the pore, and the pore lining also thickens and becomes stickier (a process called hyperkeratinisation). Together, that creates the perfect airless, oily plug for Cutibacterium acnes (the bacterium formerly called Propionibacterium acnes) to multiply in, which triggers the inflammation that turns a clogged pore into a red, sore lump.

This is why hormonal acne looks and feels different from the surface-level congestion of teenage combination-skin acne. Because the trigger is happening deeper in the gland and the inflammatory response is strong, lesions tend to sit under the skin as cysts or nodules rather than presenting as blackheads and small whiteheads. They are often painful to the touch days before they are even visible, and they take longer to resolve — one to three weeks is common, versus days for a superficial pimple.

The androgen link also explains the classic distribution. Jawline, chin, and along the sides of the lower face have a higher density of androgen-responsive oil glands than the forehead or nose, which is why hormonal breakouts cluster there even when the rest of the face is relatively clear.

## Why the timing is so predictable

Oestrogen has an anti-androgen, oil-suppressing effect on the skin. Progesterone, by contrast, can thicken the pore lining and slightly increase oil viscosity. In a typical menstrual cycle, oestrogen rises through the first half and peaks around ovulation, then both oestrogen and progesterone rise through the luteal phase before dropping sharply just before your period starts. That pre-period drop in oestrogen — combined with a relative increase in the ratio of androgens to oestrogen — is what triggers the classic "flare the week before my period" pattern that so many people recognise in themselves.

The same underlying mechanism explains other common hormonal-acne timing patterns:

- Perimenopause, when oestrogen starts declining unevenly while androgen production stays relatively steady, shifting the ratio in favour of androgens.
- Coming off combined hormonal contraception, which had been suppressing androgen effects on the skin, so acne that was controlled can return, sometimes worse than before, as the body's own hormonal rhythm reasserts itself.
- Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), where higher circulating androgens are part of the underlying hormonal profile, often alongside irregular cycles, and acne here tends to be more persistent and less cyclical than the classic pre-period flare.
- Starting or switching a progestin-only contraceptive, since some progestins have more androgenic activity than others.

## Evidence-based treatment approaches

Topical treatment is the sensible starting point for almost everyone, and it remains useful even if you go on to add a hormonal or oral therapy, because it addresses the clogging and inflammation side of the equation directly.

1. A topical retinoid (adapalene is available without a prescription in South Africa; tretinoin needs one) normalises how skin cells shed inside the pore, which is one of the two core mechanisms driving hormonal acne.
2. Benzoyl peroxide reduces the C. acnes bacterial load and helps prevent the antibiotic resistance that can develop when antibiotics are used alone.
3. Salicylic acid, a beta hydroxy acid, is oil-soluble and can penetrate into the pore itself to help clear congestion, making it a useful adjunct particularly for jawline and chin breakouts.
4. Azelaic acid is a gentler option that has anti-inflammatory and mild antibacterial action and is well tolerated even on sensitised skin.

When breakouts are clearly cyclical, deep, and concentrated along the jawline despite a good topical routine, that is the signal to see a dermatologist about systemic options rather than cycling through more over-the-counter products. Combined oral contraceptives containing certain progestins are a first-line prescription option for suitable candidates, because they directly reduce circulating free androgens. Spironolactone, an anti-androgen medication originally developed for blood pressure, is widely used off-label for hormonal acne in adult women and works by blocking androgen receptors in the skin. Both require a doctor to weigh up your personal health history, so this is not a self-prescribing decision.

## Common mistakes people make

- Over-cleansing or scrubbing the jawline harder because it "feels oily" — this strips the skin barrier and tends to increase compensatory oil production and irritation, making the pattern worse.
- Treating a hormonal flare the same as a single surface pimple by spot-treating aggressively — deep cystic lesions rarely respond well to being picked at or dried out with strong alcohol-based spot treatments, and this raises the risk of scarring.
- Switching products every one to two weeks out of frustration. Retinoids and other actives typically need six to twelve weeks of consistent use before you can fairly judge whether they are working, because a full skin cell turnover cycle takes about a month.
- Assuming diet alone explains everything. There is a genuine, moderate link between high-glycaemic diets and dairy intake and acne severity for some people, but for a hormonally driven pattern, diet changes alone rarely resolve it without also addressing the skin-barrier and androgen side.
- Stopping treatment the moment skin clears. Hormonal acne is a pattern, not a one-off event, and maintenance therapy (a lower-frequency retinoid, for example) is usually needed to keep it from returning.

## Key facts

- Acne affects a large proportion of adult women in their twenties, thirties and forties, and a distinctly jawline-and-chin pattern is one of the most recognised presentations of adult-onset acne.
- A full skin cell turnover cycle is roughly 28 days, which is why topical actives need at least six to eight weeks before their effect can be fairly assessed.
- Deep hormonal lesions often take one to three weeks to fully resolve, compared with days for a typical surface whitehead.
- Combined oral contraceptives and spironolactone are both established, guideline-recognised options for hormonal acne in appropriate candidates, used under medical supervision.
- Benzoyl peroxide and topical retinoids remain first-line, dermatologist-recommended topical treatments for most inflammatory acne, hormonal or not.

## What this means for your skin

If you notice a pattern — breakouts that consistently appear in the same jaw-and-chin zone, roughly a week before your period, and that feel more like sore lumps than surface spots — you are very likely dealing with a hormonally influenced pattern rather than a hygiene or product problem. The practical implication is that your routine needs two layers working together: a topical layer that keeps pores clear and inflammation down every day of the month, and, if the pattern is significant, a conversation with a doctor about whether addressing the hormonal driver directly makes sense for you. Trying to out-cleanse or out-scrub a hormonal pattern will generally backfire by damaging the skin barrier, which then adds barrier-related irritation on top of the original acne.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant if you are an adult experiencing breakouts concentrated on the lower face and jawline, if your skin was clear through your teens and acne only appeared or worsened in your twenties or later, if breakouts track with your menstrual cycle, if you have PCOS or irregular cycles, or if acne returned or worsened after stopping hormonal contraception. It is also relevant during perimenopause, a life stage where hormonal acne is frequently under-recognised because people assume acne is a teenage problem only.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Adapalene (topical retinoid, available over the counter in South Africa at 0.1 percent)
- Tretinoin (prescription-strength topical retinoid)
- Benzoyl peroxide, typically in 2.5 to 5 percent leave-on or wash-off formats
- Salicylic acid (beta hydroxy acid) cleansers, toners and leave-on treatments
- Azelaic acid, generally in 10 to 20 percent formulations
- Niacinamide, useful as a supportive, barrier-friendly ingredient alongside stronger actives
- Non-comedogenic, oil-free moisturisers to offset the dryness that actives like retinoids and benzoyl peroxide commonly cause
- Broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher, essential because retinoids increase sun sensitivity

## The South African context

South Africa's high UV index for most of the year makes sun protection non-negotiable for anyone using retinoids, benzoyl peroxide or acids for hormonal acne, since all three increase photosensitivity and the risk of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, which tends to be more visible and more stubborn on melanin-rich skin. That hyperpigmentation risk is a genuinely important consideration for many South African skin tones, and it is a good reason to treat lesions early rather than letting them run their full inflammatory course.

Climate matters too: on the humid KwaZulu-Natal coast, sebum production tends to run higher, which can make hormonal breakouts feel more relentless, while the drier Highveld climate can make retinoid- or benzoyl peroxide-related dryness more noticeable and may call for a richer moisturiser layered underneath actives. Locally, adapalene, salicylic acid cleansers, azelaic acid and benzoyl peroxide formulations are widely available at Clicks and Dis-Chem without a prescription, while Dermastore and Takealot stock a broader range of dermatologist-brand options and prescription-strength products can be obtained through a GP or dermatologist and dispensed at any pharmacy.

## Frequently asked questions

- Can I get hormonal acne if I am not on my period anymore? Yes — perimenopause and menopause involve their own hormonal shifts that can trigger new or worsening acne, sometimes for the first time in a person's life.
- Will cutting out dairy and sugar cure hormonal acne? It may help reduce severity for some people alongside proper skincare, but it is unlikely to resolve a genuinely hormonally driven pattern on its own.
- How long before I see improvement from a new routine? Give any consistent topical routine six to twelve weeks before judging it, since skin needs a full turnover cycle or more to show real change.
- Is hormonal acne a sign something is medically wrong with me? Not necessarily — mild cyclical breakouts are extremely common and normal, but persistent, severe, or rapidly worsening acne, especially with irregular periods, excess hair growth or other symptoms, is worth discussing with a doctor to rule out PCOS or other hormonal conditions.

## What to do this week

1. Track your breakouts against your cycle for one month to confirm whether the pattern is genuinely cyclical.
2. Introduce one active at a time — start with a low-strength adapalene or salicylic acid product rather than combining several new actives at once.
3. Switch to a gentle, non-stripping cleanser and stop any physical scrubs on the jawline.
4. Add a daily broad-spectrum SPF 30-plus, non-negotiable if you are using a retinoid or acid.
5. If breakouts are deep, cystic, and clearly cycle-linked despite good topical care, book a consultation with a dermatologist or gynaecologist to discuss hormonal options.
6. Resist picking at or aggressively spot-treating deep lesions — cool compresses and patience reduce scarring risk far more effectively.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
  ARRAY[$skt$Hormonal acne is driven by androgen sensitivity in oil glands, producing deep jawline and chin breakouts.$skt$, $skt$It commonly flares the week before a period, during perimenopause, or after stopping hormonal contraception.$skt$, $skt$Topical retinoids, benzoyl peroxide, salicylic acid and azelaic acid are solid first-line treatments.$skt$, $skt$Persistent cyclical cases often benefit from prescription options like spironolactone or combined oral contraceptives.$skt$]::text[],
  $skt$Hormonal Acne$skt$,
  $skt$SkinLabs Editorial Team$skt$,
  $skt$$skt$,
  $skt$2026-08-11$skt$::date,
  $skt$11 min read$skt$,
  2015,
  $skt$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1628682814595-a3f0816b25ff?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80$skt$,
  $skt$Portrait of a woman with dark skin tone and red lipstick$skt$,
  $skt$Ben Masora$skt$,
  $skt$https://unsplash.com/@benmasora?utm_source=SkinLabs&utm_medium=referral$skt$,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  $skt$Hormonal Acne: Causes, Triggers and Treatments$skt$,
  $skt$Why hormonal acne flares on the jawline and chin, what drives it, and which treatments actually work, from topicals to prescription options.$skt$,
