INSERT INTO public.news_articles (
  slug, title, excerpt, body_markdown, key_takeaways, sa_context_tag,
  source_name, source_url, publish_date, reading_time, word_count,
  cover_image_url, cover_image_alt, cover_credit_name, cover_credit_url,
  inline_images, seo_title, seo_description, json_ld, view_count, is_premium, status
) VALUES (
  $skt$cystic-acne-deep-treatments$skt$,
  $skt$Cystic Acne: Why Deep Breakouts Need More Than Skincare$skt$,
  $skt$Cystic acne forms deep in the skin and generally needs prescription-strength treatment, not just topical products.$skt$,
  $skb$Cystic acne is the deepest, most inflamed form of acne, and because the damage happens far below the skin's surface, it genuinely needs a treatment plan built around prescription-strength medicine rather than shelf products alone.

## Quick answer

- Cystic acne forms when a clogged, inflamed follicle ruptures deep in the dermis, spreading inflammation into surrounding tissue.
- It presents as large, painful, fluid- or pus-filled lumps that can take weeks to resolve and frequently leave lasting scars.
- Over-the-counter actives can support a routine but rarely control true cystic acne on their own.
- Oral antibiotics, hormonal therapy, and isotretinoin are the established medical-grade treatments, chosen based on severity and individual factors.
- Early, proper treatment is the single biggest factor in preventing permanent scarring.

## What makes a cyst different from a pimple

A standard pimple is a relatively contained, superficial infection and inflammation of a single pore. A cyst is what happens when that inflammatory process goes deeper and the follicle wall actually ruptures under the pressure of trapped oil, dead cells and bacteria. Once the follicle wall breaks, its contents — oil, bacterial debris, dead cells and inflammatory cells — spill into the surrounding dermis, the living layer of skin below the epidermis. The body responds to this as it would to any deep injury, with a strong immune and inflammatory reaction that walls off the area, causing the large, tender, sometimes fluctuant (fluid-shifting) lump that defines a cyst or nodule.

This is why cystic lesions are so much more painful than surface acne, sit so much deeper, and are so much slower to resolve — often two to four weeks compared with a matter of days for a simple whitehead. It is also why cystic acne carries a materially higher risk of scarring: because the inflammation damages the collagen and structural tissue of the dermis itself, not just the pore lining, the skin's healing response can leave behind an indented (atrophic) scar, or occasionally a raised (hypertrophic or keloid) one, particularly on darker skin tones and on the jawline, chest and back where skin tension is higher.

Nodules and cysts are technically distinct — a nodule is solid, a cyst has a fluid or semi-fluid centre — but in everyday use, and in most treatment decisions, they are grouped together as "nodulocystic acne" because they respond to the same treatment approach and carry the same scarring risk.

## Why over-the-counter treatment usually falls short

Topical actives like benzoyl peroxide, salicylic acid, and retinoids work primarily at or near the skin's surface: reducing surface bacteria, unclogging pores, and normalising cell turnover in the upper follicle. Cystic acne's problem, by contrast, is happening in the deeper dermis, where topical products have very limited ability to penetrate in a therapeutic concentration. This is not a failure of the products — they are doing what they are designed to do — it is simply a mismatch between where the disease process lives and where a cream or gel can reach.

This is the core reason dermatologists treat significant nodulocystic acne systemically, meaning treatment that works through the whole body via the bloodstream, rather than relying on what can be applied to the surface.

## The medical treatment ladder

1. Oral antibiotics (commonly doxycycline or minocycline) are often used as a first step to bring down the bacterial load and inflammation quickly, usually alongside a topical retinoid and benzoyl peroxide, and typically for a limited course of a few months rather than indefinitely, to reduce the risk of antibiotic resistance.
2. Hormonal therapies — combined oral contraceptives or spironolactone — are considered for people (typically women) whose cystic acne has a hormonal driver, and can be used alongside or instead of antibiotics.
3. Isotretinoin (the active ingredient once widely known by an older brand name) is the most effective treatment available for severe, scarring, or treatment-resistant nodulocystic acne. It works on essentially every mechanism that drives acne at once: it dramatically shrinks oil gland size and output, normalises how cells shed inside the follicle, reduces C. acnes bacteria indirectly by changing the follicle environment, and has a direct anti-inflammatory effect. A typical course runs four to six months and in a large proportion of patients produces long-term or permanent clearance.
4. Intralesional corticosteroid injections, done in a dermatologist's rooms, can rapidly shrink a single large, painful cyst within a day or two — genuinely useful for an urgent single lesion (before an event, for instance) but not a substitute for treating the underlying pattern.

Isotretinoin carries real, well-documented considerations — it is not appropriate during pregnancy and requires reliable contraception for anyone who can become pregnant, it needs monitoring of blood lipids and liver function during treatment, and it commonly causes significant dryness of the skin and lips. None of this is a reason to avoid it when it is genuinely indicated; it is a reason it must be prescribed and monitored by a doctor, never self-sourced.

## Common mistakes with cystic acne

- Trying to pop or squeeze a cyst. Because the lesion sits deep and its contents are not sitting in a neat surface pore, squeezing tends to push inflammatory material further into the surrounding tissue, increasing both pain and scarring risk rather than resolving anything.
- Relying only on spot treatments and waiting to "see if it goes away" for months before seeking help. Every month a cystic pattern goes untreated is additional scarring risk, so earlier medical input generally means a better long-term skin outcome.
- Stopping oral antibiotics or hormonal treatment as soon as skin looks better, rather than completing the course a doctor has set out, which can allow the pattern to rebound.
- Using very harsh, drying, DIY approaches (excessive alcohol-based toners, baking soda, toothpaste) on cystic lesions, which irritates the skin barrier without addressing the deep inflammatory process at all.
- Assuming isotretinoin is only a "last resort for the worst cases" and therefore delaying a dermatology referral — for genuinely scarring, persistent nodulocystic acne, earlier referral tends to produce a better outcome with less permanent scarring.

## Key facts

- Nodulocystic lesions can take two to four weeks to fully resolve, compared with roughly a week for a typical inflammatory pimple.
- A standard isotretinoin course runs approximately four to six months and produces long-term clearance in a majority of appropriately selected patients.
- Isotretinoin is teratogenic (harmful to a developing foetus), which is why pregnancy prevention protocols are a mandatory part of prescribing it to anyone who can become pregnant.
- Oral antibiotic courses for acne are generally kept to three to four months to limit resistance development.
- Scarring risk rises the longer active, untreated cystic inflammation persists in one area of skin.

## What this means for your skin

If you are dealing with lesions that are large, deep, painful, slow to heal, and leaving marks or pits behind, treating this purely with over-the-counter products is likely to under-treat the condition and cost you time during which scarring can accumulate. The practical shift in mindset is to see cystic acne as a medical condition that responds to systemic treatment, in the same way you would not expect a topical cream alone to treat a deep infection elsewhere in the body. A good skincare routine still matters as a supportive layer — gentle cleansing, a non-comedogenic moisturiser, daily SPF — but it should be paired with, not substituted for, appropriate medical treatment.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant if you have lumps under the skin that are painful, larger than a typical pimple, and slow to resolve; if you are noticing scarring or persistent dark or red marks after breakouts heal; if acne is concentrated on the jaw, chest or back in this deep form; or if you have already tried consistent over-the-counter treatment for several months without meaningful improvement. It is also particularly relevant for anyone with a family history of severe acne or scarring, since genetics play a meaningful role in acne severity.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Oral isotretinoin (prescription only, dispensed under a monitored programme)
- Oral doxycycline or minocycline (prescription antibiotics)
- Spironolactone and combined oral contraceptives (prescription hormonal options)
- Intralesional corticosteroid injections (in-office dermatology procedure)
- Topical retinoids (adapalene, tretinoin) as supportive maintenance therapy
- Benzoyl peroxide as a supportive topical alongside oral antibiotics
- Gentle, fragrance-free, non-comedogenic moisturisers to manage treatment-related dryness
- Broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher, essential during isotretinoin or antibiotic use due to increased photosensitivity

## The South African context

Isotretinoin, oral antibiotics and hormonal therapies are all available in South Africa through dermatologists and GPs, and isotretinoin prescribing typically requires baseline and follow-up blood tests, which are standard practice at pathology labs countrywide. Because South Africa's UV index runs high for most of the year, and both isotretinoin and oral antibiotics significantly increase photosensitivity, strict daily SPF use during treatment is essential to avoid painful sunburn and to limit post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation, which can be more pronounced and longer-lasting on melanin-rich skin. Access to dermatologists varies by province, with more specialists concentrated in Gauteng and the Western Cape; where a dermatologist isn't immediately accessible, a GP can initiate first-line treatment such as oral antibiotics or arrange referral. Locally, medical aid formularies generally cover isotretinoin and oral antibiotics with the correct prescription, though it is worth confirming authorisation requirements with your specific scheme before starting a course.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is isotretinoin the same as the old drug people used to worry about? The active ingredient is the same one used for decades under various brand names; modern prescribing includes structured monitoring that has made it a well-understood, carefully managed treatment.
- Can I use retinol from a normal skincare aisle instead of prescription treatment? Over-the-counter retinol is a much weaker, different molecule from prescription retinoids and cannot substitute for isotretinoin in genuinely cystic, scarring acne.
- Will my cystic acne scars fade on their own? Red or dark marks (post-inflammatory marks, not true scars) often fade over months, but textural, pitted scarring generally needs in-office treatments like microneedling, lasers or fillers to improve.
- How soon should I see a dermatologist about a cyst? If a lesion is large, very painful, or part of a recurring pattern rather than a one-off, book sooner rather than later — earlier treatment meaningfully reduces total scarring.

## What to do this week

1. Book a consultation with a dermatologist or GP if you have recurring large, painful lumps rather than typical surface pimples.
2. Stop attempting to squeeze or manually extract any cystic lesion.
3. Apply a cool compress to an acutely painful cyst for short-term comfort while you arrange proper treatment.
4. Keep using a gentle cleanser and non-comedogenic moisturiser as supportive care, without adding multiple new harsh actives at once.
5. Start a daily broad-spectrum SPF 30-plus if you are not already using one, particularly important once any prescription treatment begins.
6. Ask your doctor directly about the treatment ladder — antibiotics, hormonal options, or isotretinoin — so you understand which tier is appropriate for your specific case.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
