  $skt$fungal-acne-malassezia-folliculitis$skt$,
  $skt$Fungal Acne Explained: Why It Is Not Acne At All$skt$,
  $skt$Fungal acne is a yeast overgrowth in hair follicles that needs antifungal treatment, not standard acne products.$skt$,
  $skb$Fungal acne is not acne at all — it is an overgrowth of yeast in the hair follicles that produces small, uniform, itchy bumps, and it needs an entirely different treatment approach from bacterial acne or it will simply not improve.

## Quick answer

- Fungal acne (Malassezia folliculitis) is caused by an overgrowth of a naturally occurring yeast, not by bacteria or clogged pores in the usual acne sense.
- It shows up as small, uniform, often itchy bumps, typically across the forehead, chest, back and shoulders.
- Standard acne treatments like benzoyl peroxide and antibiotics do not work, and antibiotics can actually make it worse by killing off competing bacteria.
- Antifungal ingredients (ketoconazole, zinc pyrithione, and diluted antifungal shampoo left on as a mask) are the effective treatment route.
- It thrives in heat, humidity, sweat and occlusion, all of which make it particularly common in the South African summer.

## What Malassezia folliculitis actually is

Malassezia is a genus of yeast that lives naturally on everyone's skin as part of the normal microbiome, usually causing no problems at all. Under certain conditions — heat, humidity, excess oil, tight or non-breathable clothing, prolonged sweat, or a disrupted skin barrier — this yeast can overgrow inside hair follicles, and the follicle responds with an itchy, inflamed rash of small bumps. This is technically a folliculitis (inflammation of the hair follicle), not acne, even though it is very commonly mistaken for acne because of how it looks at a glance.

The key clinical differences from typical acne are worth learning to spot, because they change the whole treatment plan:

- Uniformity: fungal acne bumps tend to be strikingly similar in size to one another, whereas acne usually shows a mix of blackheads, whiteheads, and inflamed spots of varying sizes.
- Itch: fungal acne is frequently itchy, sometimes intensely so, while bacterial acne is typically tender or sore rather than itchy.
- Distribution: it favours the forehead, hairline, chest, back and shoulders — areas with more oil glands and, often, more occlusion from clothing or hair — and is less likely to show the comedones (blackheads and whiteheads) typical of ordinary acne.
- Response to treatment: this is the most reliable real-world clue. If a "stubborn acne" pattern has not budged after months of typical acne treatment, or has gotten worse on antibiotics, fungal folliculitis becomes much more likely.

## Why standard acne treatment fails, or backfires

Benzoyl peroxide and topical or oral antibiotics target bacteria, particularly C. acnes. Malassezia is a fungus, not a bacterium, so antibacterial treatments have essentially no direct effect on it. Worse, oral or topical antibiotics can disrupt the normal bacterial population on the skin that would otherwise help keep yeast populations in check, which can allow Malassezia to overgrow even further — a genuine case of the "treatment" actively worsening the underlying problem. This is why a pattern that gets worse specifically after starting an antibiotic course is a strong clue that the real diagnosis is fungal, not bacterial.

Retinoids and comedone-clearing acids can sometimes offer marginal, incidental improvement by keeping follicles less congested, but they do not address the yeast overgrowth itself and are not a reliable fix on their own.

## Confirming the diagnosis and what actually treats it

A dermatologist can confirm Malassezia folliculitis with a simple in-office look under a Wood's lamp or, in some cases, a skin scraping examined under a microscope, though many cases are diagnosed clinically based on appearance, distribution, itch, and treatment history. Because self-diagnosis is common and the treatment differs so completely from standard acne care, getting a professional opinion is genuinely worthwhile if you are not certain.

The core treatment strategy targets the yeast directly:

1. Topical antifungal creams containing ketoconazole or clotrimazole, applied to affected areas as directed, typically for several weeks.
2. Antifungal shampoo containing ketoconazole or zinc pyrithione, used off-label as a leave-on mask on the body and face — applied to damp skin, left for five to ten minutes, then rinsed off, a few times a week during an active flare and less often for maintenance.
3. Oral antifungal medication (such as fluconazole or itraconazole), prescribed by a doctor for more widespread or stubborn cases that do not respond adequately to topical treatment.
4. Zinc pyrithione or selenium sulfide-containing body washes, which can be used regularly as both treatment and prevention, particularly on the chest and back.

Alongside direct antifungal treatment, the environmental side matters just as much: Malassezia feeds on lipids, particularly certain fatty acids, so switching away from heavy, oil-rich (especially certain fatty-acid-heavy) skincare and body products during an active flare, choosing lighter, non-comedogenic and ideally "anti-fungal-acne-friendly" formulations, and showering promptly after sweating all reduce the conditions the yeast thrives in.

## Common mistakes people make

- Treating it as regular acne for months with benzoyl peroxide, salicylic acid cleansers, and topical antibiotics, none of which target yeast, leading to frustration and a pattern that seems permanently "resistant" to treatment.
- Starting an oral antibiotic for what looks like acne, not realising this can worsen a fungal pattern by disrupting the skin's normal bacterial balance.
- Using heavy, oil-based moisturisers or hair products (particularly silicone- and certain fatty-acid-rich haircare that runs onto the forehead and back) that feed the yeast.
- Wearing sweaty workout clothing for extended periods after exercise, which creates the warm, damp, occluded environment Malassezia favours, especially on the back, chest and shoulders.
- Giving up on antifungal treatment too early — visible improvement often takes two to four weeks of consistent use, and stopping at the first sign of improvement can allow a quick relapse.

## Key facts

- Malassezia is present on essentially everyone's skin as part of the normal microbiome; folliculitis only develops when conditions favour overgrowth.
- Fungal folliculitis is frequently misdiagnosed as acne, partly because the two can occur on the same person at the same time.
- Ketoconazole shampoo used as a leave-on mask is one of the most widely recommended and accessible over-the-counter-adjacent treatments.
- Improvement from consistent antifungal treatment is typically seen within two to four weeks.
- Heat, humidity, occlusive clothing, heavy sweating and oil-rich skincare are the most consistently cited environmental triggers.

## What this means for your skin

If you have a stubborn, itchy, uniform bump pattern on your forehead, chest or back that has not responded to — or has worsened with — standard acne products or antibiotics, it is worth strongly considering that you may be dealing with fungal folliculitis rather than acne. The practical shift is to stop layering on more acne-fighting products and instead simplify your routine, remove heavy oils and certain fatty-acid-rich ingredients from your skincare and haircare, and introduce a targeted antifungal approach. Because the two conditions can genuinely coexist, don't be surprised if a dermatologist recommends treating both processes at once with a combination approach.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant if you have small, itchy, uniform bumps on the forehead, chest, back or shoulders that have not responded to typical acne treatment; if your "acne" got noticeably worse after starting an antibiotic; if breakouts flare consistently after workouts, hot weather, or heavy sweating; or if you use a lot of oil-based haircare products that could be running onto your forehead or back. It is also worth considering if you live somewhere hot and humid for extended periods of the year.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Ketoconazole (topical cream or shampoo, used as leave-on treatment)
- Zinc pyrithione (shampoo or body wash)
- Selenium sulfide (shampoo or body wash)
- Clotrimazole (topical antifungal cream)
- Fluconazole or itraconazole (oral antifungals, prescription only)
- Lightweight, non-comedogenic, low-fatty-acid moisturisers and sunscreens formulated to be less "yeast-feeding"
- Sulfur-based cleansers, which some people find helpful as a gentle adjunct

## The South African context

South Africa's climate is genuinely a significant risk factor here: the humid subtropical conditions along the KwaZulu-Natal coast and the hot, sweaty summers experienced across most of the country create exactly the warm, damp environment Malassezia favours, and cases of fungal folliculitis often noticeably increase in frequency and severity over summer months. Anyone who trains outdoors, wears sports kit for extended periods, or commutes in heat and then delays showering is at higher practical risk. On the treatment side, ketoconazole shampoo and zinc pyrithione shampoos are widely and affordably available at Clicks, Dis-Chem and most supermarket pharmacy sections, making the leave-on-mask approach one of the more accessible fixes available locally without needing a prescription; oral antifungals and topical antifungal creams require a script from a GP or dermatologist.

## Frequently asked questions

- How do I tell fungal acne apart from regular acne myself? Look for unusually uniform bump size, itchiness rather than soreness, and a location on the forehead, chest or back that has not improved with standard acne products — but a dermatologist's assessment is the reliable way to know for sure.
- Can I get fungal acne and regular acne at the same time? Yes, the two conditions can coexist, which is part of why misdiagnosis is common and why a professional opinion is genuinely useful.
- Is ketoconazole shampoo safe to use on my face? It is commonly used off-label this way and is generally well tolerated for short contact periods, but patch-test first and avoid the eye area.
- How long until fungal acne clears with treatment? Most people see meaningful improvement within two to four weeks of consistent antifungal treatment, though maintenance use may be needed to prevent recurrence in hot, humid conditions.

## What to do this week

1. Review your skincare and haircare for heavy oils or fatty-acid-rich ingredients that could be reaching your forehead, chest or back.
2. Pick up a ketoconazole or zinc pyrithione shampoo and try it as a five-to-ten-minute leave-on mask two to three times this week.
3. Shower and change out of sweaty clothing as soon as possible after exercise rather than staying in damp kit.
4. Pause any oral antibiotic-based acne treatment and speak to your doctor if you suspect a fungal rather than bacterial pattern.
5. Switch to a lightweight, non-comedogenic moisturiser and sunscreen while the flare is active.
6. If there is no improvement after three to four weeks of consistent antifungal treatment, book a dermatology consultation to confirm the diagnosis.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
