  $skt$seborrheic-dermatitis-face$skt$,
  $skt$Seborrheic Dermatitis on the Face: Managing a Condition That Comes Back$skt$,
  $skt$Seborrheic dermatitis causes greasy flaking on oil-rich areas and needs ongoing management rather than a one-time cure.$skt$,
  $skb$Seborrheic dermatitis is a chronic, relapsing form of eczema that shows up as greasy, flaking, sometimes red or itchy patches wherever your skin has the most oil glands, and it responds well to targeted antifungal and anti-inflammatory treatment even though it can't be permanently cured.

## Quick answer

- Seborrheic dermatitis causes greasy-looking scale and redness on oil-gland-dense areas like the scalp, eyebrows, sides of the nose, and behind the ears.
- It's linked to an overgrowth of Malassezia yeast interacting with skin oil and an individual's inflammatory response, not poor hygiene.
- It's a chronic condition that flares and settles over time rather than something that's cured once and never returns.
- Antifungal shampoos and creams, plus mild anti-inflammatory treatment for flares, form the core, well-established treatment approach.
- Stress, cold dry weather, and hormonal changes are common, recognised flare triggers.

## What is actually happening in the skin

Seborrheic dermatitis sits in an interesting overlap zone between eczema and a mild fungal condition. The current understanding is that it develops from an interaction between the naturally occurring Malassezia yeast on everyone's skin, the amount and composition of sebum (skin oil) in a given area, and an individual's own inflammatory and immune response to that yeast's byproducts. People with seborrheic dermatitis aren't necessarily carrying more yeast than anyone else — the more consistent finding is that their skin reacts more strongly to it, producing the inflammation, redness and characteristic greasy scale.

This explains the classic distribution: seborrheic dermatitis appears almost exclusively in areas dense with sebaceous (oil) glands — the scalp, eyebrows and the space between them, the sides of the nose and nasolabial folds, behind and inside the ears, the central chest, and sometimes the eyelid margins (where it's called blepharitis) and beard area in men. In infants, the same process produces the harmless, self-resolving condition known as cradle cap.

The scale itself tends to look distinctly different from other scalp and facial conditions — greasy, yellowish, and somewhat waxy in texture, rather than the drier, whiter flaking of simple dandruff (which is generally considered a much milder version of the same underlying process) or the thicker, more defined, silvery scale of psoriasis. This visual distinction is genuinely useful for telling the conditions apart, though overlap and lookalike presentations do happen, which is why a dermatologist's opinion is worthwhile when it's unclear.

## Why it flares and what makes it worse

Seborrheic dermatitis is chronic and relapsing by nature, meaning it tends to improve with treatment and then return periodically rather than disappearing permanently after one course of treatment. Several well-recognised factors influence how often and how severely it flares.

Cold, dry weather is one of the most consistently reported triggers, which is why many people notice their scalp and facial flaking worsen distinctly in winter. Stress is another well-documented trigger, likely acting through its broader effects on immune and inflammatory activity. Hormonal changes, including those around puberty (when sebum production increases significantly) and other periods of hormonal flux, also influence flare frequency and severity, which is part of why the condition often first appears or worsens during adolescence and young adulthood.

Certain underlying health conditions are associated with more severe or harder-to-control seborrheic dermatitis, including Parkinson's disease and HIV, likely related to how these conditions affect immune function and, in Parkinson's, possibly sebum composition — this doesn't mean everyone with seborrheic dermatitis has an underlying condition, but a sudden, severe, hard-to-control case in someone without a prior history is one of the reasons a doctor might look a little more broadly at overall health.

## Evidence-based treatment options

The treatment approach targets the yeast component and the inflammatory component together, and because it's a chronic condition, ongoing maintenance treatment rather than a single course is the realistic long-term plan.

1. Antifungal shampoos and washes containing ketoconazole, zinc pyrithione, selenium sulfide, or ciclopirox are the foundation of treatment for the scalp, and can also be used carefully on facial and body areas as a wash or brief leave-on mask.
2. Mild topical corticosteroids or, for facial and more sensitive areas, topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus or pimecrolimus) are used for short bursts during active flares to bring down redness and itch quickly, without the long-term steroid-related risks that come with continuous facial steroid use.
3. Topical antifungal creams (ketoconazole or ciclopirox cream) are used on facial and body areas for both active flares and, at a reduced frequency, ongoing maintenance to prevent recurrence.
4. A gentle, non-irritating skincare routine that avoids very rich, occlusive products on affected areas, since heavier formulations can worsen the greasy scale, while overly harsh or stripping products can worsen barrier irritation.
5. For the scalp specifically, using an antifungal shampoo consistently a few times a week even once flares have settled — as maintenance rather than only during flares — meaningfully reduces how often the condition returns.

Because seborrheic dermatitis is chronic, the realistic goal of treatment is good long-term control rather than a one-time cure, and most people settle into a maintenance routine using antifungal products at a lower frequency once the initial flare is under control.

## Common mistakes people make

- Treating it as regular dandruff and only using a generic anti-dandruff shampoo without addressing facial or other body areas that may also be affected.
- Stopping treatment entirely the moment flaking and redness clear, rather than shifting to a lower-frequency maintenance routine, which often leads to a faster-than-expected relapse.
- Using very rich, oil-heavy moisturisers or hair oils on affected areas, believing dryness is the core problem, when in fact the greasy, oil-rich environment is part of what sustains the yeast overgrowth.
- Using strong topical steroids continuously on the face for extended periods to manage ongoing flares, which risks skin thinning, visible blood vessels, and steroid-related rosacea-like reactions over time.
- Assuming a stubborn facial rash is definitely seborrheic dermatitis without ruling out psoriasis, eczema or fungal infection, especially when the pattern doesn't respond to standard treatment as expected.

## Key facts

- Seborrheic dermatitis affects a substantial proportion of adults at some point, with cradle cap representing a very common, self-resolving infant version.
- It's more common and often more severe in men than in women, likely related to differences in sebum production.
- Antifungal shampoos are typically used two to three times a week during an active flare, then reduced to weekly or fortnightly for maintenance.
- Topical calcineurin inhibitors are specifically useful for facial seborrheic dermatitis because they avoid the skin-thinning risk of long-term facial steroid use.
- Flares are most commonly reported in winter and during periods of high stress.

## What this means for your skin

If you have greasy, yellowish flaking with redness in the classic zones — scalp, eyebrows, sides of the nose, behind the ears — the practical approach is to think in terms of ongoing management rather than a single fix. That means building an antifungal shampoo or wash into your regular routine even when skin looks clear, keeping facial and body moisturisers on the lighter side in affected areas, and having a mild anti-inflammatory option on hand from a doctor for when a flare does happen, rather than escalating to a strong steroid and using it indefinitely.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant if you have persistent greasy scaling and redness on the scalp, eyebrows, sides of the nose, ears, or central chest; if your "dandruff" hasn't responded to standard anti-dandruff shampoo; if flares reliably worsen in winter or during stressful periods; or if you have an eyelid margin irritation that keeps recurring. It's also worth extra attention if you have an underlying condition affecting immune function, since seborrheic dermatitis can be more severe and harder to control in that context.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Ketoconazole (shampoo and cream)
- Zinc pyrithione (shampoo)
- Selenium sulfide (shampoo)
- Ciclopirox (shampoo and cream)
- Mild topical corticosteroids for short-term flare control
- Tacrolimus or pimecrolimus (topical calcineurin inhibitors for facial use)
- Lightweight, non-comedogenic, non-oily moisturisers for affected facial areas
- Salicylic acid-based scalp treatments to help lift thicker scale before antifungal treatment

## The South African context

South Africa's cooler, drier Highveld winters are a genuine trigger period for many people with seborrheic dermatitis, with flaking and redness commonly worsening from around May through August, while the more humid coastal regions may see somewhat steadier symptoms year-round. The strong, consistent South African sun exposure most of the year can sometimes mildly improve seborrheic dermatitis symptoms for some people, though this is not a substitute for proper treatment and unprotected sun exposure carries its own risks, so it shouldn't be relied on as a treatment strategy. On the access side, ketoconazole, zinc pyrithione and selenium sulfide shampoos are widely and affordably available at Clicks, Dis-Chem and most supermarket pharmacy counters without a prescription, making the maintenance side of treatment genuinely easy to sustain locally; topical calcineurin inhibitors and stronger prescription options are available through a GP or dermatologist.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is seborrheic dermatitis the same as dandruff? They're related and considered part of the same spectrum, with dandruff generally regarded as a milder, scalp-only version and seborrheic dermatitis involving more inflammation and often other body areas too.
- Can seborrheic dermatitis be cured permanently? No, it's a chronic condition that's managed rather than cured, though most people achieve good long-term control with consistent maintenance treatment.
- Is it caused by poor hygiene? No, it's related to yeast overgrowth and an individual's inflammatory response, not cleanliness, and over-washing can actually irritate the skin further.
- Why does my seborrheic dermatitis get worse in winter? Cold, dry air is a well-documented trigger, likely through its effects on skin barrier function and possibly on yeast activity.

## What to do this week

1. Start or restart an antifungal shampoo (ketoconazole, zinc pyrithione or selenium sulfide) two to three times this week if you have an active flare.
2. Check other classic areas — eyebrows, sides of the nose, behind the ears, chest — for symptoms you may not have connected to your scalp flaking.
3. Switch to lighter, non-oily moisturisers on affected facial areas rather than rich creams or oils.
4. If facial redness and flaking are significant, book a GP or dermatologist appointment to discuss a short course of mild topical treatment.
5. Plan to continue antifungal shampoo at a reduced, maintenance frequency even once flaking clears, rather than stopping completely.
6. Note whether flares track with stress or season, so you can start maintenance treatment proactively ahead of your typical flare period.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
