) VALUES (
  $skt$atopic-dermatitis-eczema-basics$skt$,
  $skt$Atopic Dermatitis Basics: Why Eczema Management Starts With the Barrier$skt$,
  $skt$Eczema stems from a weaker skin barrier and an overactive immune response, so daily barrier care is the real foundation.$skt$,
  $skb$Atopic dermatitis, commonly called eczema, is a chronic condition rooted in a genetically weaker skin barrier and an overactive immune response, which means effective management is built around repairing and protecting that barrier every single day, not just treating flares when they appear.

## Quick answer

- Atopic dermatitis is driven by a combination of a genetically compromised skin barrier and an immune system that overreacts to normally harmless triggers.
- It typically causes dry, intensely itchy, inflamed patches, often in the creases of elbows and knees in older children and adults, and on the face and outer limbs in babies.
- Daily moisturising with a thick, fragrance-free emollient is the single most important, evidence-backed foundation of management.
- Topical corticosteroids and, for more involved cases, topical calcineurin inhibitors or newer targeted treatments manage active flares.
- It's part of the "atopic triad" alongside asthma and hay fever, and often runs in families.

## The barrier-and-immune-system story behind eczema

Atopic dermatitis has two intertwined root causes, and understanding both explains almost every piece of standard treatment advice you'll come across. The first is a structural one: many people with atopic dermatitis have a genetic variation affecting filaggrin, a protein essential for building and maintaining the outermost layer of skin, the stratum corneum, which acts as the body's primary barrier against water loss and against irritants, allergens and microbes getting in. With a less effective filaggrin-built barrier, skin loses moisture more easily (transepidermal water loss) and lets outside irritants and allergens penetrate more readily than in skin with normal barrier function.

The second piece is immunological: that easier penetration of allergens and irritants, combined with an underlying tendency toward an overactive type of immune response (called a Th2-skewed response), leads the immune system to react to normally harmless substances — dust, certain foods in some cases, pollen, pet dander, even the skin's own bacteria — with an inflammatory response that produces the redness, swelling and, critically, the intense itch that defines eczema. That itch then drives scratching, which further damages an already compromised barrier, letting in more irritants and triggering more inflammation — a genuinely vicious cycle often called the "itch-scratch cycle," and breaking it is one of the central goals of treatment.

This dual mechanism is also why atopic dermatitis so often appears alongside asthma and allergic rhinitis (hay fever) in the same person or same family — together they're referred to as the atopic triad, reflecting a shared underlying tendency toward this type of allergic, barrier-related immune response, even though each condition affects a different organ system.

## How the pattern changes across a lifetime

Atopic dermatitis's typical distribution shifts noticeably with age, which is genuinely useful for recognising it at different life stages. In infants, it commonly appears on the face, scalp and outer surfaces of the arms and legs, often starting somewhere between two and six months old. As children get older, the pattern typically shifts to the classic flexural distribution — the creases of the elbows, behind the knees, wrists, and ankles — which tends to persist into adulthood for those who continue to have the condition. In adults, eczema can also appear on the hands, eyelids, and neck, and can present with thickened, leathery skin (lichenification) in areas that have been chronically scratched or rubbed over a long period.

A meaningful proportion of children with atopic dermatitis see significant improvement or full resolution by adolescence, though a substantial number continue to experience it, in a milder or more localised form, into adulthood.

## The core, evidence-based treatment framework

Eczema management rests on a small number of well-established principles, most of which are directed at the barrier side of the equation first, with anti-inflammatory treatment layered in for active flares.

1. Daily moisturising with a thick, fragrance-free emollient, applied generously at least once and ideally twice a day, is the single most consistently recommended foundation of eczema management, because it directly addresses the barrier dysfunction that underlies the whole condition.
2. Lukewarm (not hot) showers or baths, kept relatively short, followed by moisturising within a few minutes while skin is still slightly damp — a technique often called "soak and seal" — helps lock in hydration more effectively than moisturising on completely dry skin.
3. Topical corticosteroids, matched in strength to the severity and location of the flare (milder formulations for the face and skin folds, stronger ones for thicker-skinned areas like the palms or soles), remain a first-line, well-established treatment for active flares.
4. Topical calcineurin inhibitors (tacrolimus, pimecrolimus) are steroid-free anti-inflammatory options particularly useful for sensitive areas like the face and eyelids, or for longer-term use where continuous steroid use isn't appropriate.
5. For moderate to severe cases not adequately controlled by topical treatment, newer systemic options including biologic medications and oral JAK inhibitors, prescribed and monitored by a dermatologist, have significantly expanded what's possible for people with more extensive disease.
6. Identifying and reducing exposure to personal triggers — this varies hugely between individuals but commonly includes certain fabrics (wool in particular), fragranced products, excessive hand-washing without adequate moisturising, and, for some, specific food allergens, though food triggers are more relevant in a subset of cases, particularly in young children, and should be explored with a doctor rather than through broad, unsupervised elimination diets.

## Common mistakes people make

- Using moisturiser only when skin already looks or feels dry, rather than daily as a preventive, barrier-supporting habit regardless of how skin looks on a given day.
- Being afraid of topical steroids ("steroid phobia") and under-using them during a genuine flare, which often leads to the flare persisting longer and skin damage accumulating rather than clearing efficiently with correctly dosed, appropriately monitored use.
- Taking very long, hot showers or baths, which strips natural oils and can worsen dryness and itch despite feeling temporarily soothing.
- Trying broad, unsupervised food elimination diets without a doctor's guidance, based on the assumption that food is definitely the trigger, which can lead to unnecessary nutritional restriction without addressing the actual drivers.
- Scratching or allowing prolonged scratching to continue unaddressed, without also treating the itch directly (through antihistamines where appropriate, cool compresses, and proper anti-inflammatory treatment), letting the itch-scratch cycle continue and the barrier damage compound.

## Key facts

- Atopic dermatitis affects a very large proportion of children globally, with a meaningful proportion continuing to experience symptoms into adulthood.
- The filaggrin gene variation associated with atopic dermatitis also increases the risk of asthma and hay fever, explaining the atopic triad pattern.
- Daily emollient use has been shown consistently to reduce flare frequency and severity, making it the single most evidence-backed baseline intervention.
- Topical corticosteroid strength should be matched to body area — thinner-skinned areas like the face and eyelids need much milder formulations than thicker areas like palms or soles.
- Biologic and oral JAK inhibitor treatments represent a genuinely newer treatment category for moderate to severe eczema not controlled by topical treatment alone.

## What this means for your skin

If you or your child has recurring dry, itchy, inflamed patches, particularly in the classic elbow and knee creases, the most impactful daily habit is consistent moisturising, applied generously and often, rather than treating it as an optional extra only for dry days. Building a "soak and seal" routine after bathing, choosing fragrance-free products across the board, and having an appropriately strength-matched topical steroid ready for flares (used correctly and for the recommended duration, not indefinitely avoided out of fear) together form the realistic, evidence-based foundation of long-term control. For anyone whose eczema is extensive or not responding to this foundation, newer systemic treatments genuinely change what's achievable and are worth discussing with a dermatologist.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone with a personal or family history of eczema, asthma or hay fever; for infants and children with persistent dry, itchy rashes in the classic locations for their age; for adults with recurring hand, eyelid or flexural eczema; and for anyone whose eczema is significantly affecting sleep, daily comfort or quality of life, which is a recognised indicator that it's time to escalate beyond basic moisturising to a doctor-guided treatment plan.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Thick, fragrance-free emollients and ointments (petrolatum-based options are often the most effective for very dry, compromised skin)
- Ceramide-containing moisturisers, which directly support the skin's own barrier lipids
- Topical corticosteroids matched in strength to body area and flare severity
- Tacrolimus or pimecrolimus (topical calcineurin inhibitors)
- Colloidal oatmeal-based baths or washes for itch relief
- Oral antihistamines for itch and sleep disruption during flares, used under guidance
- Biologic and oral JAK inhibitor medications for moderate to severe, treatment-resistant cases (prescription, specialist-managed)

## The South African context

South Africa's climate variation genuinely affects eczema management: the dry Highveld winters commonly worsen eczema through lower humidity and increased barrier moisture loss, meaning many people need to step up moisturising frequency and richness specifically over the colder months, while the more humid KwaZulu-Natal coast can bring its own challenges with heat, sweat and increased irritation in skin folds. South Africa's notably hard water in some regions can also contribute to skin dryness and barrier irritation, which is worth factoring in when eczema seems to worsen regardless of product changes. On the access side, fragrance-free emollients and colloidal oatmeal products are available at Clicks and Dis-Chem, with a wider specialist range through Dermastore and Takealot; topical steroids, calcineurin inhibitors and newer systemic treatments require a prescription from a GP or dermatologist, with biologic and JAK inhibitor treatments generally managed by a dermatologist given their cost and monitoring requirements.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is eczema contagious? No, it's not an infectious condition and cannot be passed to another person through contact.
- Will my child grow out of eczema? A significant proportion of children see major improvement or full resolution by adolescence, though some continue to experience it, often in milder form, into adulthood.
- Are topical steroids dangerous for long-term eczema use? Used correctly, at the right strength for the area and duration recommended by a doctor, they're a well-established, effective treatment; the real risk lies more in under-treating flares out of unwarranted fear than in appropriate steroid use.
- Can diet cure my eczema? For most people, no — daily barrier care and appropriate anti-inflammatory treatment matter more than diet, though a subset of people, particularly young children, do have genuine food triggers worth exploring with a doctor's guidance.

## What to do this week

1. Start applying a thick, fragrance-free moisturiser at least once, ideally twice, daily regardless of how skin currently looks.
2. Switch to lukewarm, shorter showers and moisturise within a few minutes of getting out while skin is still slightly damp.
3. Identify and reduce any personal triggers you've noticed, such as certain fabrics or fragranced products.
4. If you're avoiding a prescribed topical steroid out of concern, discuss correct use and duration with your doctor rather than under-treating an active flare.
5. Track flare timing against season, stress and any dietary changes to help identify personal patterns.
6. If eczema is extensive, disrupting sleep, or not responding to consistent barrier care, book a dermatology consultation to discuss escalating treatment options.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
