) VALUES (
  $skt$rosacea-type-1-erythematotelangiectatic$skt$,
  $skt$Rosacea Type 1: Why Your Face Won't Stop Flushing$skt$,
  $skt$Type 1 rosacea is a vascular condition causing persistent redness and visible thread veins, not dirt or bacteria, and needs calming care.$skt$,
  $skb$Rosacea Type 1, known medically as erythematotelangiectatic rosacea, is a chronic condition in which the small blood vessels of the face become permanently dilated and overreactive, producing persistent redness, flushing and visible thread veins rather than the bumps and pustules people usually associate with rosacea.

## Quick answer

- Type 1 rosacea is about vascular reactivity, not bacteria or dirt — your facial blood vessels dilate too easily and too often.
- The hallmark signs are persistent central facial redness, a tendency to flush intensely, and visible thread-like veins (telangiectasia) around the nose and cheeks.
- It is a lifelong, manageable condition, not an infection, and it cannot be scrubbed, exfoliated or "detoxed" away.
- Triggers are highly individual but commonly include heat, sun, spicy food, alcohol, exercise and emotional stress.
- Treatment focuses on trigger management, vascular-calming skincare, and in-clinic vessel treatments where needed — not harsh actives.

## What is actually happening under the skin

Rosacea Type 1 sits at the mild end of a spectrum of facial vascular dysfunction. The blood vessels just beneath the surface of your cheeks, nose, chin and forehead are meant to dilate and constrict in response to temperature, exercise or emotion, then settle back to baseline. In erythematotelangiectatic rosacea, that "settle back" mechanism is faulty. The vessels dilate too easily, stay dilated too long, and over years some of them lose their elasticity entirely and remain permanently visible as telangiectasia — the fine red or purple thread veins that tend to concentrate around the nasal sidewalls and upper cheeks.

There is a strong neurovascular component here. Nerve endings in rosacea-prone skin appear to be more sensitive to heat and certain chemical triggers, releasing inflammatory and vasodilating signals more readily than in non-rosacea skin. This is why a flush can come on almost instantly after a hot drink, a glass of wine, or stepping from an air-conditioned room into Highveld midday heat — the trigger doesn't need to touch the skin, it just needs to reach the nervous system that controls those vessels. The skin barrier in rosacea-prone individuals is also frequently compromised, meaning it loses water more easily and reacts more sharply to environmental stress, which sets up a feedback loop: barrier damage triggers redness, and the products used to treat redness can further irritate an already fragile barrier if chosen poorly.

Genetics play a meaningful role. Rosacea is more common in those with fair, sun-sensitive skin (Fitzpatrick I to III), and it tends to run in families, particularly those of Celtic or Northern European descent, though it is diagnosed across all skin tones and is frequently under-recognised in darker skin because the redness reads as a subtler brown or violet discolouration rather than the bright pink seen in fair skin. It typically first appears in your late twenties to forties and, without management, tends to progress gradually rather than resolve on its own.

## The trigger map: why flushing feels so unpredictable

One of the most frustrating aspects of Type 1 rosacea is that triggers vary from person to person and can even shift over a lifetime. That said, a well-established shortlist accounts for the majority of flares:

- Heat exposure — hot showers, saunas, direct sun, and simply being in a hot room or car
- Spicy food and hot beverages, which trigger a nerve-mediated flush independent of temperature
- Alcohol, particularly red wine, which dilates vessels directly
- Intense exercise, especially in heat or humidity
- Emotional stress and embarrassment, which triggers a vascular flush via the autonomic nervous system
- Cold wind followed by rapid rewarming, common on Cape winter mornings
- Certain skincare ingredients, especially high concentrations of alcohol, fragrance, menthol or physical scrubs

Keeping a simple trigger diary for two to three weeks — noting flares against food, weather, stress and skincare changes — remains one of the most useful diagnostic tools a person can use themselves, because it turns a vague sense of "my face just does this" into an actionable pattern.

## Evidence-based approaches that actually help

Because Type 1 rosacea is vascular rather than infective, the treatment logic is different from acne-focused regimens. The goals are threefold: reduce the frequency and intensity of flushing, calm background inflammation and barrier fragility, and address visible vessels that have already become permanent.

Topical options with reasonable evidence include azelaic acid, which has anti-inflammatory and mild vessel-calming properties and is generally well tolerated; niacinamide, which supports barrier function and has been shown to reduce transepidermal water loss and background redness over consistent use; and topical brimonidine or oxymetazoline, vasoconstrictor treatments available on prescription that temporarily narrow dilated vessels for cosmetic improvement over several hours (these treat the appearance, not the underlying vascular tendency, and can occasionally cause rebound redness if overused).

For the permanent thread veins themselves, topical treatment has limited effect — vessels that have lost their ability to constrict need to be addressed with light-based treatments such as pulsed dye laser or intense pulsed light (IPL), performed by a qualified practitioner, which selectively targets the haemoglobin in dilated vessels and causes them to collapse and be reabsorbed. This is considered the most effective option for visible telangiectasia and often requires two to four sessions spaced several weeks apart, with results that are long-lasting though not always permanent, since new vessels can form over years.

Daily sun protection is non-negotiable, not optional, because UV exposure is one of the most consistent flare triggers and also accelerates the vessel damage that leads to permanent telangiectasia. A broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50, ideally a mineral formulation with zinc oxide, is generally better tolerated than chemical filters in rosacea-prone skin, since some chemical UV filters themselves can trigger stinging and flushing.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Reaching for exfoliating acids or physical scrubs to try to "smooth out" the redness, which further damages an already compromised barrier and worsens flushing.
2. Using hot water to cleanse, which feels soothing in the moment but is a direct vascular trigger.
3. Layering multiple new active ingredients at once when the skin flares, making it impossible to identify what helped or hurt.
4. Assuming redness is rosacea without ruling out other causes such as an allergic contact reaction, seborrhoeic dermatitis or simply sun damage, all of which can mimic Type 1 rosacea.
5. Giving up on skincare entirely out of fear of triggering a flare, which leaves the skin barrier unsupported and often makes background redness worse over time.
6. Expecting a single product to eliminate visible thread veins, when only in-clinic vascular treatment can meaningfully reduce established telangiectasia.

## Key facts

- Rosacea affects an estimated one in ten adults globally to some degree, with Type 1 being the most common subtype.
- Fitzpatrick skin types I to III are most frequently diagnosed, though rosacea occurs in all skin tones.
- Typical onset is between the late twenties and mid-forties.
- Pulsed dye laser and IPL are considered the gold-standard treatments for established telangiectasia.
- Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is considered a core, non-negotiable part of management, not an optional extra.
- Topical vasoconstrictors provide temporary redness reduction lasting several hours per application, not a permanent fix.

## What this means for your skin

If you have Type 1 rosacea, your daily routine should be built around calming and protecting rather than actively treating. That means a gentle, fragrance-free cleanser used with lukewarm rather than hot water, a barrier-supporting moisturiser containing ceramides or niacinamide, and daily mineral sunscreen as the last step every single morning regardless of weather. Introduce any new product one at a time and give it at least two to three weeks before judging it, since rosacea-prone skin reacts faster to irritation than it does to genuine improvement. Avoid physical exfoliation altogether, and be cautious with any product marketed as "brightening" or "resurfacing," since these often contain the acids and fragrances that trigger flushing. If you want to address existing thread veins, that conversation belongs with a dermatologist or accredited laser practitioner rather than a skincare product.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant if you have fair-to-medium skin that flushes easily with heat, alcohol or spicy food, if you have a family history of rosacea, or if you have noticed persistent central facial redness that does not fully fade between episodes. It is also relevant for anyone who has been told they have "sensitive skin" that reacts to almost everything, since undiagnosed rosacea is frequently mislabelled this way. People with darker skin tones should pay particular attention too, since the condition is under-diagnosed in Fitzpatrick IV to VI skin, where the redness can present as a duskier brown-red or violet tone rather than classic pink, and is sometimes mistaken for melasma or simple sun damage.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Azelaic acid, typically in 10 to 20 percent formulations, for anti-inflammatory and mild vascular benefits
- Niacinamide, for barrier repair and reduction of background redness
- Ceramide-based moisturisers to reinforce a compromised skin barrier
- Mineral (physical) sunscreens containing zinc oxide or titanium dioxide
- Centella asiatica and panthenol, both traditionally used for their calming, barrier-supportive properties
- Prescription topical vasoconstrictors such as brimonidine or oxymetazoline for temporary redness reduction
- Green-tinted colour correctors as a cosmetic camouflage option on flare days

## The South African context

South Africa's climate works against Type 1 rosacea in several specific ways. The Highveld's intense midday UV index, which regularly reaches extreme levels in summer, is both a direct flare trigger and a long-term accelerant of vessel damage, making daily SPF genuinely more important here than in cooler, lower-UV climates. Coastal humidity in places like Durban adds a second layer of challenge, since heat and humidity together are a classic flushing trigger, while the drier Highveld air can leave an already compromised barrier more prone to stinging and tightness. Load shedding and the resulting loss of aircon or fans during hot spells is a very real, very local trigger that many South Africans with rosacea don't think to log in their trigger diary. On the retail side, mineral sunscreens and azelaic acid formulations are increasingly available through Clicks, Dis-Chem and Dermastore, and it is worth specifically asking pharmacy staff for "rosacea-safe" or "sensitive skin" ranges, since these stores typically stock dedicated sections for reactive skin. IPL and pulsed dye laser treatment for visible vessels is offered by dermatologists and accredited aesthetic clinics in most major centres, though availability of pulsed dye laser specifically is more limited outside Johannesburg, Cape Town and Durban, so IPL is often the more accessible option elsewhere in the country.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is rosacea the same as just having sensitive skin? No — sensitive skin is a general reactivity, while rosacea is a specific chronic vascular condition with defined patterns, though the two can overlap and rosacea is often mistaken for simple sensitivity.
- Will rosacea go away on its own? No, it is a chronic condition that tends to persist or gradually progress without management, though flare frequency and severity can be significantly reduced with consistent trigger avoidance and appropriate skincare.
- Can I still wear makeup with rosacea? Yes — mineral-based, fragrance-free makeup is generally well tolerated, and green colour correctors can help visually neutralise redness on harder days.
- Does diet cause rosacea? Diet does not cause rosacea, but specific foods and drinks, particularly spicy food, hot beverages and alcohol, are well-documented triggers for flushing episodes in people who already have it.

## What to do this week

1. Start a simple daily trigger diary noting weather, food, stress and any flare-ups, even briefly.
2. Switch to lukewarm water for cleansing and avoid hot showers directed at the face.
3. Check your current sunscreen and moisturiser for fragrance and alcohol, and consider a mineral SPF 30 to 50 if you are not already using one.
4. Pause any physical scrubs or high-strength acids for two weeks and note whether redness settles.
5. If thread veins are visible and bothering you, book a consultation with a dermatologist or accredited laser clinic to discuss IPL or pulsed dye laser options.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
