  $skt$melasma-triggers-and-heat$skt$,
  $skt$Why Heat (Not Just Sun) Triggers Melasma$skt$,
  $skt$Melasma is driven by UV, visible light and heat together, which is why sunscreen alone often isn't enough to control it.$skt$,
  $skb$Melasma is triggered not only by UV light but also by heat and visible light, which is why it can worsen even with diligent sunscreen use if that sunscreen isn't formulated to block the full spectrum of light and thermal exposure that drives it.

## Quick answer

- Melasma is a pigmentation disorder in which patches of brown or greyish discolouration appear, usually on the cheeks, forehead, upper lip and chin.
- Heat itself, independent of UV, stimulates pigment-producing cells and can trigger or worsen melasma, a phenomenon called thermal or infrared-induced pigmentation.
- Visible light, including light from screens and bright indoor lighting, is now understood to worsen melasma in medium-to-dark skin tones, not just UV.
- Hormonal factors, especially oestrogen fluctuation from pregnancy, contraceptives or hormone therapy, are major underlying drivers.
- Effective management requires broad-spectrum, tinted sunscreen, heat avoidance, and targeted pigment-regulating actives, with realistic expectations around gradual, ongoing control rather than a permanent cure.

## Why heat is such an underrated trigger

Most people understand melasma as sun-triggered, and while UV exposure is undeniably central, heat exposure independent of UV is a well-established and frequently overlooked trigger. Melanocytes, the pigment-producing cells in the skin, are stimulated not only by UV radiation but by rising skin temperature itself. Infrared radiation, which makes up a large portion of sunlight's warming effect but does not carry the same photobiological damage as UV, appears to activate inflammatory and pigment pathways in the skin when temperatures rise for sustained periods. This explains a pattern many melasma sufferers have long reported anecdotally: their pigmentation flares after hot yoga, cooking over a stove, sitting near a heater, taking long hot showers, or simply living through a heatwave — all situations involving heat but not necessarily direct UV exposure.

This matters practically because a person can be diligent about applying sunscreen and still see melasma worsen if they spend time in consistently hot environments, since sunscreen (even a good one) does very little to block heat itself. It also explains why melasma tends to flare more in summer even in people who are conscientious about shade and hats, and why it can worsen during activities like intensive exercise, sauna use, or standing over a hot stove for long cooking sessions, which are heat-heavy but low-UV scenarios.

## The visible light and hormone connection

Alongside heat, research over the past decade has clarified that visible light — the light our eyes actually see, including bright indoor lighting and, to a lesser but real degree, the blue light emitted by phone and laptop screens — can stimulate pigment production in melasma-prone skin, particularly in Fitzpatrick skin types IV to VI. This is a critical piece of the puzzle for South African skin, since a large proportion of the population sits within this range and standard UV-only sunscreens (particularly older chemical formulations) do very little to block visible light. Tinted mineral sunscreens, which contain iron oxides, are specifically formulated to filter visible light in addition to UV and are now considered a meaningful upgrade for melasma-prone skin rather than a cosmetic nicety.

Hormones remain a foundational driver of melasma, which is why the condition is sometimes still referred to by its older name, "the mask of pregnancy." Oestrogen and progesterone appear to sensitise melanocytes, making them more reactive to whatever triggers — UV, heat, visible light or even skin inflammation from other conditions — a person is exposed to. This is why melasma frequently appears or worsens during pregnancy, with hormonal contraceptive use, or during hormone replacement therapy, and why it can partially or fully improve after childbirth or stopping hormonal medication, though it very often does not disappear completely and tends to reactivate with future hormonal shifts or sun exposure.

## Building a genuinely comprehensive management plan

Because melasma has multiple overlapping triggers, effective management requires addressing more than one pathway at once, and expectations need to be realistic: melasma is a chronically recurring condition that is managed and controlled rather than cured outright.

Sun and light protection form the non-negotiable foundation. This means a broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50 applied daily and reapplied through the day, ideally a mineral or hybrid formulation containing iron oxides for visible light protection, combined with physical barriers like wide-brimmed hats and seeking shade during peak UV hours. Heat avoidance, while less discussed, deserves equal attention: keeping showers lukewarm rather than hot, avoiding prolonged direct exposure to stove heat or heaters, and being mindful during intense exercise or sauna sessions in hot climates.

On the active ingredient side, several options have solid evidence for reducing existing pigmentation and preventing new patches from forming. Hydroquinone, used under professional guidance and typically in short cycles rather than continuously, remains one of the most effective depigmenting agents by directly inhibiting the enzyme responsible for melanin production. Tranexamic acid, both topical and in some cases prescribed orally by a dermatologist, has emerged as a particularly useful option because it works partly by calming the vascular and inflammatory signalling that feeds melasma, addressing a different part of the mechanism than pigment inhibitors alone. Azelaic acid, niacinamide, vitamin C and cysteamine cream are other evidence-supported options, generally gentler and suited to longer-term maintenance use between more intensive treatment cycles. In-clinic treatments such as chemical peels and certain laser or light-based therapies can help, but must be approached cautiously in melasma, since aggressive or poorly matched treatments can trigger inflammation that worsens pigmentation rather than improving it — this is a condition where more aggressive is not automatically better.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Relying only on chemical sunscreen without visible-light protection, missing a major trigger pathway in medium-to-dark skin.
2. Ignoring heat exposure entirely, assuming sun is the only relevant environmental trigger.
3. Using hydroquinone continuously and indefinitely without a break, which can increase the risk of a rare but serious complication called ochronosis, a paradoxical darkening of the skin.
4. Seeking aggressive laser or peel treatments without professional guidance, risking a flare of post-inflammatory pigmentation on top of existing melasma.
5. Expecting a permanent cure rather than ongoing management, leading to frustration and treatment-hopping when melasma predictably recurs after a hormonal shift or unprotected sun exposure.
6. Under-applying sunscreen — most people apply far less than the amount used in testing, significantly reducing real-world protection.

## Key facts

- Melasma affects women far more often than men, and is especially prevalent in people with Fitzpatrick skin types III to VI.
- Visible light exposure has been shown to induce pigmentation in darker skin types even without UV present.
- Tinted mineral sunscreens containing iron oxides provide meaningfully better visible-light protection than untinted formulas.
- Tranexamic acid, topical and oral, has grown into a mainstream treatment option over the past decade.
- Hydroquinone is typically used in supervised cycles of around three months on, with a break, rather than continuously.
- Melasma frequently recurs after pregnancy, contraceptive changes or sun exposure even after successful treatment.

## What this means for your skin

If you have melasma or are prone to it, your daily non-negotiables are a tinted, broad-spectrum mineral or hybrid sunscreen reapplied through the day, genuine heat awareness (lukewarm showers, shade during the hottest hours, caution with sauna and intense stovetop cooking), and a consistent pigment-regulating active such as azelaic acid, niacinamide or a dermatologist-guided tranexamic acid or hydroquinone protocol. Patience is essential — visible improvement in melasma typically takes eight to twelve weeks at minimum, and full results from a professional treatment plan can take several months, with maintenance needed indefinitely to prevent recurrence, particularly through hormonal changes or high-UV seasons.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone with Fitzpatrick skin types III to VI who has noticed symmetrical brown or greyish patches on the cheeks, forehead, upper lip or chin, particularly if patches worsen in summer, with hormonal contraceptive use, during pregnancy, or after time in hot environments. It is also relevant for pregnant women and those on hormonal contraception generally, given how strongly hormonal fluctuation predisposes to melasma, and for anyone who has previously had melasma improve and wants to prevent recurrence.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Tinted mineral sunscreen with iron oxides, for combined UV and visible light protection
- Hydroquinone, prescription-strength and cycle-based, under professional supervision
- Tranexamic acid, topical or prescribed oral, for its anti-pigment and anti-inflammatory action
- Azelaic acid, for gentler ongoing pigment regulation
- Niacinamide, to support barrier function and reduce pigment transfer
- Vitamin C (L-ascorbic acid or stable derivatives), as an antioxidant with mild brightening effect
- Cysteamine cream, a newer non-hydroquinone depigmenting option

## The South African context

South Africa's exceptionally high UV index for much of the year, particularly across the Highveld and interior in summer, makes melasma both more common and harder to control than in lower-UV climates, and the population's broad representation across Fitzpatrick III to VI skin types means visible-light-driven melasma is a genuinely significant, not niche, concern here. Hot Highveld summers and humid coastal heat both increase the thermal trigger pathway, meaning South Africans with melasma need to think seriously about heat exposure beyond sun exposure alone — this includes practical local realities like standing over a stove during load shedding-driven candlelit cooking, or working in un-airconditioned spaces during heatwaves. On the positive side, tinted mineral sunscreens and tranexamic acid-containing serums are increasingly available through Dis-Chem, Clicks and Dermastore, and dermatologists in major centres are well versed in melasma management, including access to prescription-strength hydroquinone and, where appropriate, oral tranexamic acid. It is worth specifically seeking out "tinted" or "iron oxide" sunscreen rather than any broad-spectrum product, since this detail is often the single biggest upgrade available without a prescription.

## Frequently asked questions

- Can melasma be completely cured? Not reliably — it is generally a chronic, recurring condition that responds well to management but tends to reactivate with hormonal shifts or unprotected UV and heat exposure.
- Does melasma only happen during pregnancy? No, though pregnancy is a common trigger due to hormone fluctuation, melasma also occurs with hormonal contraceptive use, hormone therapy, and sometimes with no identifiable hormonal trigger at all, especially in sun-heavy climates.
- Is regular sunscreen enough to prevent melasma flares? Often not enough on its own — untinted sunscreens don't block visible light, which is a known trigger in medium-to-dark skin, so a tinted, iron-oxide-containing formula offers more complete protection.
- Can exercise make melasma worse? Yes, intense exercise that raises skin temperature significantly, especially in hot conditions, can act as a heat-based trigger independent of sun exposure.

## What to do this week

1. Check your current sunscreen for iron oxide or tint, and switch to a tinted mineral or hybrid formula if it isn't already.
2. Start reapplying sunscreen at least once during the day, not only in the morning.
3. Take note of heat exposure this week — hot showers, cooking, sauna, exercise in heat — and see if patterns line up with pigment changes.
4. Add one evidence-based pigment-regulating active, such as azelaic acid or niacinamide, if you aren't using one already.
5. If melasma is significant or distressing, book a consultation with a dermatologist to discuss a supervised hydroquinone or tranexamic acid protocol.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
