  $skt$post-inflammatory-hyperpigmentation-pih$skt$,
  $skt$Post-Inflammatory Hyperpigmentation: Why Marks Outlast the Pimple$skt$,
  $skt$PIH is flat pigment left behind after inflammation, not a scar, and it fades with consistent sun protection and pigment-regulating care.$skt$,
  $skb$Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation is the flat, brown or dark mark that lingers after a pimple, cut or irritation has healed, and it happens because your skin's pigment-producing cells overreact to inflammation, not because the mark itself is a scar.

## Quick answer

- PIH is a flat discolouration, ranging from light brown to almost black depending on skin tone, that appears after any skin inflammation or injury has resolved.
- It is not a scar — there is no textural change or tissue damage, only excess pigment deposited in the skin.
- It is far more common and more pronounced in medium-to-dark skin tones (Fitzpatrick IV to VI), where melanocytes are more reactive to inflammation.
- Sun exposure is the single biggest factor in how dark and how long-lasting a mark becomes.
- Fading is achievable with consistent sun protection and pigment-regulating actives, but takes months, not days, and picking or over-treating the area makes it worse.

## Why dark skin is more prone to leaving marks

Post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation happens because melanocytes — the cells that produce melanin, the pigment that gives skin its colour — become hyperactive in response to any inflammatory event in the skin. When a pimple, insect bite, cut, burn, or even an aggressive skincare reaction inflames the skin, the healing process releases inflammatory signalling molecules. In melanin-rich skin, these same signals also strongly stimulate melanocytes, causing them to dump extra pigment into the healing area as what is essentially an overcorrection. This is why the same pimple that leaves barely a trace in fair skin can leave a stubborn dark mark for months in medium-to-dark skin — the mechanism is the skin's own reactivity, not a difference in the severity of the original inflammation.

This distinction between PIH and scarring is one of the most important things to understand and is frequently confused. A true scar involves structural change to the skin — collagen loss (as in atrophic acne scars) or collagen overproduction (as in raised or keloid scars) — and requires resurfacing or collagen-remodelling treatments to improve. PIH, by contrast, is purely a pigment issue sitting in the upper layers of skin (epidermal PIH) or sometimes deeper (dermal PIH, which is more stubborn and slower to fade). This matters enormously for treatment choice: pigment-fading ingredients work well for PIH but do essentially nothing for a true textural scar, and conversely, resurfacing treatments aimed at scars can actually worsen PIH if they cause additional inflammation.

## The sun exposure factor and why marks seem to "never fade"

Sun exposure is the single most significant factor determining how dark a PIH mark becomes and how long it persists. UV light directly stimulates melanocyte activity, meaning that even a small amount of unprotected sun exposure on a healing mark can deepen it and effectively reset the fading timeline. This is why many people report a mark that seemed to be fading nicely, then suddenly darkening again after a weekend outdoors, a day at the beach, or simply months of daily incidental sun exposure without SPF. It is also why PIH is often more visible and persistent in summer and in sunnier climates generally.

Left completely untreated but sun-protected, epidermal PIH will usually fade on its own over three to six months as the skin naturally turns over and clears the excess pigment. Deeper dermal PIH, however, can take a year or more to fade without active treatment, and in some cases persists indefinitely if sun exposure continues unchecked. This timeline is frequently misunderstood, leading people to conclude a product "isn't working" after only two or three weeks, when realistic fading timeframes are measured in months.

## Evidence-based treatment approaches

The foundation of any PIH treatment plan, without exception, is rigorous daily sun protection. Without this, no active ingredient can outpace ongoing pigment stimulation from UV exposure — it is the single highest-leverage intervention available and is more important than any specific brightening product.

Beyond sun protection, several ingredient categories have solid evidence for fading PIH. Vitamin C, particularly stable formulations of L-ascorbic acid, works as an antioxidant and mild tyrosinase inhibitor (tyrosinase being the enzyme central to melanin production), making it a good long-term daily option. Niacinamide reduces the transfer of pigment from melanocytes to surrounding skin cells and has good evidence for gradual improvement with minimal irritation risk, making it particularly suitable for reactive or acne-prone skin already dealing with inflammation. Azelaic acid offers a dual benefit for those whose PIH stems from acne, treating both the underlying breakouts and the resulting marks simultaneously. Retinoids, including over-the-counter retinol and prescription tretinoin, accelerate skin cell turnover, which helps shift pigmented cells to the surface faster, though they require careful, gradual introduction since they can cause irritation that paradoxically triggers new PIH if used too aggressively. For deeper or more stubborn marks, a dermatologist may recommend chemical peels using glycolic or lactic acid, or in select cases, hydroquinone under supervision, though these approaches require professional guidance in medium-to-dark skin, since overly aggressive treatment can itself trigger new inflammation and worsen pigmentation — a phenomenon sometimes called treatment-induced PIH.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Picking, squeezing or scratching at healing spots, which is one of the most reliable ways to guarantee a dark mark, since physical trauma is itself a strong inflammatory trigger.
2. Skipping sunscreen on "healed" skin because the pimple itself is gone, not realising the mark left behind is still highly UV-reactive.
3. Expecting fading within days or a couple of weeks, then abandoning a genuinely effective product before it has had time to work.
4. Using aggressive at-home peels or strong acids too frequently in an attempt to speed up fading, which triggers fresh inflammation and can deepen the very mark being treated.
5. Confusing PIH with acne scarring and pursuing resurfacing treatments like microneedling or lasers meant for textural scars, which won't address flat pigment and can occasionally worsen it in reactive skin.
6. Layering too many brightening actives at once, increasing irritation risk without proportionally increasing results.

## Key facts

- PIH is significantly more common and more visually pronounced in Fitzpatrick skin types IV to VI.
- Epidermal PIH typically fades within three to six months with consistent sun protection; dermal PIH can take a year or longer.
- Daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 or higher is considered the single most important factor in PIH resolution speed.
- Niacinamide in the 4 to 5 percent range has reasonable evidence for reducing pigment transfer with low irritation risk.
- PIH is a pigment issue, not a textural scar, and does not require resurfacing treatments to resolve.
- New or worsening PIH is a common side effect of overly aggressive at-home exfoliation or peeling.

## What this means for your skin

If you're dealing with PIH, the priority order matters: sun protection first, then gentle consistent pigment-regulating actives, and patience above all. Apply broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50 every single morning without exception, including on cloudy days, and reapply if you're outdoors for extended periods. Introduce one pigment-focused active at a time — niacinamide is a sensible starting point for most skin types given its low irritation profile — and give it a genuine eight to twelve weeks before assessing results. Resist the urge to pick at active breakouts, since preventing new PIH is far easier than treating existing marks. If marks are widespread, very dark, or not improving after several months of consistent care, that's the point to bring in a dermatologist rather than escalating over-the-counter products on your own.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone with medium-to-dark skin (Fitzpatrick IV to VI) who has noticed marks lingering after acne, insect bites, cuts, or skin irritation. It is especially relevant for people managing active acne, since ongoing breakouts constantly generate new PIH even as older marks fade, making acne control itself a key part of any PIH strategy. It's also relevant for anyone recovering from an aggressive skincare reaction, sunburn, or a minor injury who wants to actively minimise the chance of a lasting mark rather than waiting to treat it after the fact.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50, mineral or chemical, applied daily without exception
- Niacinamide, for gentle, low-irritation pigment transfer reduction
- Vitamin C (stable L-ascorbic acid or derivatives), for antioxidant and mild brightening support
- Azelaic acid, particularly useful when PIH is acne-driven
- Retinoids (over-the-counter retinol or prescription tretinoin), introduced gradually for cell turnover support
- Alpha hydroxy acids such as glycolic or lactic acid, used at moderate, non-irritating strengths
- Prescription hydroquinone, for stubborn cases, used under professional supervision

## The South African context

South Africa's high year-round UV index makes PIH management genuinely harder here than in cooler, lower-UV countries, because the ongoing pigment stimulation from sun exposure works directly against fading, even during "mild" South African winters where UV can still be significant on clear Highveld days. Given the country's broad range of skin tones, PIH is an extremely common concern across South African dermatology and skincare retail, and demand has led to good local availability of niacinamide- and vitamin C-based serums through Clicks, Dis-Chem, Superbalist and Takealot, along with dedicated "dark mark" or "even tone" ranges. It is worth prioritising a genuinely broad-spectrum, high-SPF sunscreen formulated for daily wear in hot climates, since a heavy or greasy sunscreen is one that people quietly stop reapplying, undermining the single most important part of PIH treatment. For stubborn or extensive PIH, dermatologists in major South African centres are experienced in treating pigmentary concerns across a wide range of skin tones and can offer supervised peels or prescription-strength options where appropriate.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation the same as a scar? No, PIH is flat pigment discolouration with no textural change, while a true scar involves structural changes to the skin's collagen.
- How long does it take for a dark mark to fade? Epidermal PIH usually fades over three to six months with consistent sun protection, while deeper dermal PIH can take a year or more.
- Will sunscreen alone fade my marks? Sunscreen alone won't actively fade existing marks, but it is essential to prevent them from darkening further and is the foundation any other treatment needs to work properly.
- Can I use retinol and vitamin C together for PIH? Yes, though it's generally better to introduce them separately at first and consider using vitamin C in the morning and retinol at night to minimise irritation.

## What to do this week

1. Add or upgrade to a broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50 sunscreen and commit to daily use, including cloudy days.
2. Stop picking at any active breakouts or healing spots this week, even briefly, to prevent new marks forming.
3. Introduce one gentle pigment-focused active, such as niacinamide, if you aren't already using one.
4. Set a genuine eight to twelve week check-in point before judging whether a product is working, rather than assessing weekly.
5. If marks are extensive, very dark, or not improving after several months, book a consultation with a dermatologist to discuss prescription options.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
