) VALUES (
  $skt$post-inflammatory-erythema-pie$skt$,
  $skt$Post-Inflammatory Erythema: The Red Marks Acne Leaves Behind$skt$,
  $skt$PIE is flat vascular redness left after inflammation, distinct from pigment-based PIH, and needs calming, not brightening, treatment.$skt$,
  $skb$Post-inflammatory erythema is the flat pink or red mark that lingers after a pimple or skin injury has healed, and unlike a dark mark, it comes from damaged surface blood vessels rather than excess pigment, which means it needs a different treatment approach entirely.

## Quick answer

- PIE is a flat red, pink or purplish mark left behind after inflammation, caused by dilated or damaged tiny blood vessels near the skin's surface.
- It is most visible and most commonly diagnosed in fair-to-medium skin tones, where redness reads clearly against the surrounding skin.
- It is frequently confused with post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation (PIH), but the two require different treatments because one is vascular and the other is pigment-based.
- Pressing gently on the mark and watching it blanch (turn pale) briefly is a simple way to recognise PIE, since true pigment marks do not blanch.
- Sun protection, gentle skin barrier care and, for stubborn cases, vascular laser treatment are the most evidence-based paths to fading it.

## What's actually happening in the skin

When skin experiences inflammation — from a popped pimple, an aggressive extraction, a burn, a cut, or even a harsh reaction to a skincare product — the tiny capillaries near the skin's surface dilate and can become temporarily damaged or leaky as part of the body's healing and inflammatory response. In fair-to-medium skin, where this vascular change isn't masked by higher baseline melanin, the result is a flat red, pink or sometimes purplish mark that can look remarkably like a lingering pimple long after the original blemish has actually resolved. This is post-inflammatory erythema, and it is fundamentally a vascular phenomenon rather than a pigment one.

This distinction is the single most important thing to understand about PIE, because it is so commonly mistaken for post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation. Both appear after inflammation and both are flat (no texture change), which is where the confusion comes from. But PIH is caused by excess melanin deposited by overactive pigment cells, while PIE is caused by blood vessels sitting close to the surface, dilated and slow to fully constrict back to their resting state. A simple, genuinely useful home test is the blanch test: gently pressing a fingertip on the mark for a couple of seconds. If the area visibly pales and then returns to pink or red once released, that's a strong indicator the mark is vascular (PIE). Pigmented marks (PIH) do not blanch in this way, because the discolouration lives in melanin within skin cells, not in blood flow.

Getting this distinction right matters enormously for treatment choice. Pigment-fading ingredients like hydroquinone, tyrosinase inhibitors, or aggressive brightening acids do very little for genuine PIE, since there's no excess pigment to fade — you're essentially treating the wrong target and may irritate the skin without addressing the actual problem. Conversely, vascular-focused treatments do little for pure pigment-based PIH. Many people have marks that are actually a mix of both, particularly in medium-toned skin, which is part of why persistent post-acne marks can feel so stubborn and resistant to any single treatment approach.

## Why some people get PIE and others get PIH from the same breakout

Skin tone is the biggest determining factor in which type of mark a given inflammatory event tends to leave. In fair skin (Fitzpatrick I to III), where baseline melanin activity is lower, the vascular response to inflammation is more visually dominant, making PIE more commonly diagnosed and reported. In medium-to-dark skin (Fitzpatrick IV to VI), the same inflammatory event tends to trigger a much stronger melanocyte response, making PIH the more dominant and more commonly discussed outcome, though PIE absolutely still occurs in these skin tones and is simply harder to see against higher background pigment, meaning it's likely underdiagnosed rather than genuinely rare.

The severity and type of the original inflammatory event also plays a role. Deeper, more aggressively inflamed lesions — cystic acne, popped or picked-at pimples, and lesions that were physically manipulated during healing — tend to produce more pronounced vascular damage and therefore more noticeable PIE, regardless of skin tone. This is one of many reasons dermatologists consistently advise against picking or squeezing active breakouts: doing so doesn't just risk infection and scarring, it specifically increases the likelihood and severity of the residual redness that follows.

## Evidence-based treatment approaches

As with PIH, daily sun protection is foundational, because UV exposure worsens inflammation and vascular reactivity, slowing the natural fading process and potentially deepening the redness. A broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50 should be non-negotiable for anyone actively managing PIE.

Beyond sun protection, ingredients that support barrier repair and calm vascular reactivity are the most relevant category, rather than pigment-focused actives. Niacinamide has some evidence for reducing redness and supporting barrier function, making it a reasonable first addition. Centella asiatica and other soothing botanical extracts are commonly used for their calming, vessel-supportive properties, though evidence here is more traditional than robustly clinical. Vitamin C has some support for strengthening capillary walls over time, which may help vessels return to their resting state more efficiently, in addition to its antioxidant benefits. Azelaic acid, useful across both PIH and PIE given its anti-inflammatory action, is a sensible dual-purpose option when marks are of mixed type, which is common.

For genuinely stubborn or extensive PIE that isn't responding to consistent topical care over several months, vascular laser treatments — particularly pulsed dye laser, and to a lesser extent certain IPL protocols — are considered the most effective option, since they work directly on the dilated vessels causing the redness rather than relying on the skin's own gradual vascular remodelling. This is a professional, in-clinic treatment requiring a qualified practitioner and is generally reserved for cases where topical care alone hasn't resolved things after a reasonable trial period, typically several months.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Treating PIE with pigment-fading products meant for PIH, such as hydroquinone or aggressive brightening acids, which do little for vascular redness and can cause unnecessary irritation.
2. Picking or squeezing active breakouts, which significantly increases both the likelihood and severity of resulting PIE.
3. Skipping sunscreen because the mark "isn't a pimple anymore," not realising ongoing UV exposure still worsens vascular reactivity and slows fading.
4. Expecting quick results — PIE, like PIH, fades over months rather than weeks, and impatience often leads to unnecessary product switching.
5. Assuming all post-acne redness is permanent scarring rather than temporary PIE, and pursuing unnecessarily aggressive resurfacing treatments too early.
6. Over-exfoliating in an attempt to "speed up" fading, which increases inflammation and can worsen the very redness being treated.

## Key facts

- PIE is caused by dilated or damaged surface capillaries, distinct from the excess melanin responsible for PIH.
- A simple blanch test — pressing and releasing — helps distinguish vascular PIE (which pales under pressure) from pigmented PIH (which does not).
- PIE is most commonly diagnosed and most visible in fair-to-medium skin tones (Fitzpatrick I to III).
- Untreated, uncomplicated PIE often fades naturally over several months to about a year with good sun protection.
- Pulsed dye laser is considered the most effective in-clinic option for stubborn, treatment-resistant PIE.
- Picking or squeezing active breakouts measurably increases the risk and severity of resulting PIE.

## What this means for your skin

If you're dealing with what looks like lingering post-acne redness, the first useful step is figuring out whether it's actually PIE, PIH, or a mix of both, using the simple blanch test as a starting point. From there, build a routine around daily broad-spectrum SPF, a gentle barrier-supportive moisturiser, and calming actives like niacinamide, centella asiatica or azelaic acid rather than pigment-focused brightening products, unless you've confirmed a pigment component is also present. Avoid picking at any current or future breakouts, since prevention is genuinely more effective than any treatment once the mark has formed. Give any routine at least two to three months before judging results, and consider a professional consultation about vascular laser treatment if marks are extensive, longstanding, or significantly affecting confidence.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone with fair-to-medium skin who notices flat red or pink marks lingering weeks or months after acne, injury or skin irritation has otherwise healed. It's particularly relevant for people who have a habit of picking at breakouts, since this specific behaviour is one of the strongest predictors of pronounced PIE. It also matters for anyone confused about why their "dark spot" products aren't working on red marks, since this is often a sign the mark is actually vascular rather than pigmented, and for people in medium-to-dark skin who may have an underdiagnosed vascular component alongside more visible pigment marks.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50, applied daily as the non-negotiable foundation
- Niacinamide, for barrier support and mild redness reduction
- Centella asiatica (also called cica), for traditional calming and vessel-supportive benefits
- Vitamin C, for antioxidant support and capillary strength over time
- Azelaic acid, useful for mixed PIE and PIH presentations
- Fragrance-free, ceramide-based moisturisers to reduce ongoing barrier-driven inflammation
- Professional pulsed dye laser or IPL, for stubborn, treatment-resistant cases

## The South African context

South Africa's high UV index means that unprotected sun exposure will actively work against PIE fading for a larger portion of the year than in cooler climates, making daily sunscreen an even higher priority here regardless of skin tone. Given the population's mix of skin tones, both PIE and PIH are common, and mixed presentations are frequent, so it's worth being specific with a dermatologist or pharmacist about whether marks blanch under pressure, since this detail helps steer product selection correctly from the start rather than after months of the wrong routine. Calming, barrier-focused skincare containing centella asiatica and niacinamide is widely available through Clicks, Dis-Chem and Superbalist, often marketed under "redness relief" or "sensitive skin" ranges. Access to vascular laser treatment such as pulsed dye laser is more concentrated in Johannesburg, Cape Town and Durban, so those in smaller centres may need to travel or consider IPL as a more locally accessible alternative for stubborn redness.

## Frequently asked questions

- How do I know if my mark is PIE or PIH? Try the blanch test — gently press the mark; if it visibly pales and then returns to red or pink, it's likely PIE (vascular), whereas marks that don't change colour under pressure are more likely PIH (pigment).
- Will my dark spot serum fix red marks? Not effectively — pigment-focused products target melanin and do little for the dilated vessels causing PIE, so a redness-focused, barrier-supportive routine works better.
- How long does PIE take to fade on its own? With good sun protection and gentle care, uncomplicated PIE often fades over several months to around a year, though this varies by depth and severity of the original inflammation.
- Does picking at pimples really make PIE worse? Yes, physically manipulating an active breakout increases vascular trauma and inflammation, which measurably increases the likelihood and severity of resulting redness.

## What to do this week

1. Try the blanch test on any lingering red marks to establish whether they're vascular (PIE) or pigmented (PIH).
2. Add or confirm daily broad-spectrum SPF 30 to 50, applied every morning without exception.
3. Swap any pigment-focused brightening product for a calming, barrier-supportive one if your marks test as PIE.
4. Commit to not picking at any current breakouts this week, even small ones.
5. If redness is extensive or has persisted for over six months without improvement, consult a dermatologist about vascular laser treatment options.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
