  $skt$keratosis-pilaris-strawberry-skin$skt$,
  $skt$Keratosis Pilaris: What Causes 'Strawberry Skin' on Your Arms$skt$,
  $skt$Keratosis pilaris is harmless keratin buildup blocking hair follicles, not poor hygiene, and responds well to consistent exfoliation.$skt$,
  $skb$Keratosis pilaris is a common, harmless skin condition in which dead skin cells plug the hair follicles, creating the small rough bumps and mottled redness on the upper arms, thighs and cheeks often nicknamed "strawberry skin" or "chicken skin," and it is a buildup issue rather than a sign of poor hygiene.

## Quick answer

- Keratosis pilaris (KP) is caused by excess keratin blocking hair follicles, creating small, rough, skin-coloured or red bumps, most often on the upper arms, thighs and buttocks.
- It is extremely common, genetic, and not caused by poor hygiene, allergies or diet in most cases.
- It tends to worsen in winter and with dry skin, and often improves somewhat in summer or humid conditions.
- There is no permanent cure, but consistent exfoliation and moisturising can significantly improve texture and reduce redness.
- It commonly runs in families and is frequently associated with generally dry skin and, in some people, eczema.

## What's actually happening in the follicle

Keratosis pilaris develops when keratin — the tough, structural protein that makes up the outer layer of skin, hair and nails — builds up excessively around individual hair follicles instead of shedding normally from the skin's surface. This buildup forms a small, hard plug that blocks the follicle opening, creating the characteristic tiny, rough bump. Surrounding skin can appear pink or red, particularly in fair skin, either from mild irritation around the blocked follicle or from small blood vessels becoming slightly more visible in the affected area, which is part of why KP is so often described using the "strawberry skin" or "goosebump" nicknames.

The exact reason keratin overproduces or fails to shed properly in KP-prone skin isn't completely understood, but it has a strong genetic component and is closely linked with generally dry skin. People with KP often have a slightly different skin barrier function than those without it, showing a similar underlying tendency toward dryness and mild barrier dysfunction seen in conditions like atopic dermatitis (eczema) and ichthyosis vulgaris — in fact, having eczema or a family history of it meaningfully increases the likelihood of also having keratosis pilaris, though plenty of people have KP with no eczema connection at all.

Where it appears also has a predictable pattern. The backs of the upper arms are by far the most common site, followed by the front of the thighs, buttocks, and less commonly the cheeks, particularly in children, where it can sometimes be mistaken for acne or simple facial roughness. It typically first appears in childhood or adolescence, often becomes most pronounced in the teenage years, and for many people gradually improves into adulthood, though a significant proportion of adults continue to experience it lifelong, particularly during drier months.

## Why it flares with the seasons

One of the most consistent patterns people notice with keratosis pilaris is that it worsens in winter and improves, sometimes substantially, in summer. This is directly tied to ambient humidity and skin hydration. Cold, dry air — indoors from heating and outdoors from low winter humidity — strips moisture from the skin's outer layer more aggressively, which worsens the keratin buildup process and makes existing bumps feel rougher and more textured. Hot showers, more frequently used in winter, compound this by further stripping the skin's natural lipid barrier.

Conversely, warmer, more humid conditions naturally hydrate the skin more effectively, softening the keratin plugs and making bumps less prominent, which is why many people report their KP is "barely noticeable" during summer or in humid coastal climates and flares noticeably during dry winter months or after time spent in air-conditioned or heated indoor environments for extended periods.

## Evidence-based management approaches

Because keratosis pilaris has no permanent cure, management is about consistent, ongoing texture improvement rather than a one-time fix, and realistic expectations matter here — most people manage rather than eliminate KP entirely.

Chemical exfoliation is generally more effective and gentler on the skin than physical scrubbing for KP, since physical exfoliation can irritate the already sensitive bumps without effectively clearing the keratin plug itself. Alpha hydroxy acids, particularly lactic acid and glycolic acid, work by loosening the bonds between dead skin cells, helping clear the follicle blockage over consistent use. Lactic acid has the added benefit of being naturally hydrating, which suits the generally dry skin type most associated with KP. Salicylic acid, a beta hydroxy acid, is oil-soluble and can penetrate into the follicle itself more effectively than water-soluble acids, making it a particularly good choice for KP specifically, since the problem is fundamentally located inside the follicle opening.

Urea, in concentrations typically ranging from 10 to 40 percent depending on formulation and intended use, is another well-established option, working both as a gentle keratolytic (breaking down excess keratin) and as a humectant that draws moisture into the skin, addressing both the buildup and the underlying dryness simultaneously. Topical retinoids, including over-the-counter retinol and prescription tretinoin, can help by increasing skin cell turnover, though they need to be introduced gradually since KP-prone skin is often already somewhat dry and irritation-sensitive.

Consistent, generous moisturising underpins all of the above, since a well-hydrated skin barrier makes any keratolytic ingredient work more effectively and reduces the baseline dryness that feeds the condition. Thick, ceramide- or urea-containing creams applied immediately after bathing, while skin is still slightly damp, tend to be more effective than thinner lotions applied later.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Aggressively scrubbing the area with rough physical exfoliants or loofahs, which can irritate the skin and worsen redness without effectively clearing follicle blockages.
2. Expecting a permanent cure rather than ongoing, ideally lifelong, management, leading to frustration and product-hopping.
3. Using hot water for bathing, which further dries out already dry, KP-prone skin.
4. Popping or picking at individual bumps, which can cause irritation, infection risk and post-inflammatory marks without resolving the underlying keratin plug.
5. Stopping treatment as soon as skin looks better, rather than maintaining a routine, since KP reliably returns without ongoing management.
6. Assuming red, bumpy arms are always KP without considering other possibilities like folliculitis (a bacterial or fungal follicle infection) if bumps are painful, pus-filled or rapidly spreading, which would need different treatment entirely.

## Key facts

- Keratosis pilaris is estimated to affect a very large proportion of the general population to some degree, with many cases mild enough to go unmentioned to a doctor.
- It most commonly appears on the upper arms, thighs and buttocks, and occasionally the cheeks in children.
- KP is strongly linked with generally dry skin and has a higher prevalence in people with eczema or a family history of it.
- It typically first appears in childhood or adolescence and often (not always) improves gradually with age.
- Lactic acid, glycolic acid, salicylic acid and urea are the most evidence-supported topical treatment ingredients.
- Symptoms characteristically worsen in low-humidity winter conditions and improve in warmer, more humid weather.

## What this means for your skin

If you have keratosis pilaris, the most effective long-term approach is a simple, consistent routine rather than chasing a cure: a gentle chemical exfoliant containing lactic, glycolic or salicylic acid used two to four times a week on affected areas, followed by a rich, urea- or ceramide-containing moisturiser applied while skin is still damp from bathing. Switch to lukewarm rather than hot water for showers and baths, and be prepared to increase moisturising frequency during winter months when symptoms typically worsen. Give any new routine at least six to eight weeks before assessing results, since keratin turnover is a gradual process, and expect texture to improve significantly rather than disappear completely — smoother, less red skin is a realistic and achievable goal.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone who has noticed persistent small, rough bumps on the upper arms, thighs or buttocks, particularly if they run in the family or occur alongside generally dry skin or eczema. It's also relevant for parents of children or teenagers with rough, bumpy skin patches, since KP commonly first appears in this age group and is often mistaken for another skin issue. People who notice their skin texture worsening specifically in winter, or who live in drier climates or use heating extensively, are a particularly good fit for a proactive, season-adjusted management routine.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Lactic acid, typically in 5 to 12 percent over-the-counter formulations, for gentle exfoliation and hydration
- Glycolic acid, for effective chemical exfoliation of the keratin plug
- Salicylic acid, oil-soluble and able to penetrate the follicle directly
- Urea, in 10 to 40 percent concentrations depending on product type, for keratolytic and moisturising dual action
- Ceramide-based body creams and lotions, to support overall barrier function
- Topical retinoids (retinol or prescription tretinoin), for increased cell turnover, introduced gradually
- Fragrance-free, soap-free body washes to avoid additional barrier stripping

## The South African context

South Africa's climate variation plays out clearly with keratosis pilaris. The dry, cooler Highveld winter, with its low humidity and heavy reliance on indoor heating, tends to worsen KP noticeably for people living in Gauteng and similar interior regions, while the more humid coastal climate of Durban and the KwaZulu-Natal coast tends to keep skin naturally better hydrated and KP correspondingly milder for residents there, even in winter. Hard water, common in parts of South Africa's interior, can also contribute to a slightly drier skin feel after bathing, which is worth factoring in when choosing body wash and moisturising routines. Body-focused chemical exfoliants and urea creams are well stocked in Clicks and Dis-Chem, often in dedicated "rough and bumpy skin" sections, and are generally affordable and accessible compared to many facial skincare actives, making KP one of the more budget-friendly conditions to manage consistently. For families managing KP in children, South African pharmacies typically stock gentler paediatric-formulated options as well, worth asking about directly.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is keratosis pilaris contagious? No, it is a genetic, keratin-buildup condition with no infectious component whatsoever, and cannot be passed to or caught from anyone else.
- Will keratosis pilaris ever go away completely? For many people it improves significantly with age and consistent care, but it doesn't have a guaranteed permanent cure, and ongoing management is usually needed to maintain results.
- Is it okay to exfoliate keratosis pilaris every day? Daily gentle chemical exfoliation is sometimes fine depending on the product strength and your skin's tolerance, but two to four times a week is a safer general starting point to avoid irritation.
- Does diet affect keratosis pilaris? There's no well-established dietary cause for KP, and it is not typically linked to specific foods, though general skin hydration and overall health can play a supporting role.

## What to do this week

1. Switch body wash to a gentle, fragrance-free, soap-free formula if not already using one.
2. Introduce a chemical exfoliant containing lactic, glycolic or salicylic acid two to three times this week on affected areas.
3. Apply a rich, urea- or ceramide-based moisturiser immediately after bathing while skin is still slightly damp.
4. Lower shower and bath water temperature to lukewarm rather than hot.
5. Set a six to eight week reminder to properly assess texture improvement before changing products again.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
