) VALUES (
  $skt$milia-extraction-and-prevention$skt$,
  $skt$Milia: Why Those Tiny White Bumps Won't Just Wash Off$skt$,
  $skt$Milia are keratin trapped in a sealed pocket under the skin, not a pimple, and need professional extraction, not squeezing.$skt$,
  $skb$Milia are the small, firm, pearly-white bumps that appear most often around the eyes and cheeks because dead skin cells become trapped in a tiny pocket just beneath the skin's surface, and because they sit under intact skin rather than in an open pore, no amount of washing, scrubbing or squeezing will make them disappear.

## Quick answer

- Milia are small keratin-filled cysts trapped just under the surface of the skin, most common around the eyes, cheeks and eyelids.
- They are not acne, blackheads or whiteheads, and cannot be extracted or treated the same way.
- Because they sit beneath intact skin, they generally need to be professionally extracted with a sterile lancet, not squeezed at home.
- Certain heavy, occlusive skincare and eye creams can contribute to their formation, especially around the delicate eye area.
- Gentle exfoliation and lighter-weight eye and face products can help prevent new milia, though some people are simply more prone to them regardless.

## Why milia are structurally different from a pimple or blackhead

Milia form when keratin — the same structural skin protein implicated in keratosis pilaris — becomes trapped in a small pocket just beneath the outermost layer of skin, rather than within an open pore or hair follicle as with a blackhead or whitehead. This is the critical structural difference that explains almost everything else about how milia behave. A blackhead sits within an open follicle, which is why it can sometimes be gently expressed or dissolved with the right topical treatment. A milium (the singular form of milia) sits in a closed, sealed pocket with no natural opening to the skin's surface at all, which is why washing, scrubbing, steaming or standard blackhead-removal tools have essentially no effect on it — there is no follicle opening to clear because it isn't a follicle problem in the first place.

This also explains why milia feel notably firmer and more embedded than a typical whitehead when you touch them, and why they don't respond to typical acne treatments like benzoyl peroxide or salicylic acid cleansers in the way a comedonal breakout might. The keratin is sealed away from the surface, essentially in its own small capsule, and needs to be either physically released through the skin (via extraction) or gradually resolved as skin naturally exfoliates and, occasionally, the trapped material works its way out on its own over a period of weeks to months.

There are actually a few different types of milia recognised clinically. Primary milia, the most common type, form spontaneously, particularly around the eyes and cheeks, and are thought to arise directly from the sebaceous glands. Secondary milia form after some kind of skin trauma or damage — burns, blistering, dermabrasion, certain skin conditions, or even after long-term use of very heavy, occlusive skincare or steroid creams — that disrupts normal skin healing and traps keratin as the skin repairs itself. Milia are also extremely common in newborns, appearing on the nose and face shortly after birth and typically resolving entirely on their own within the first few weeks of life without any treatment at all, which is a separate and generally self-limiting situation from adult milia.

## Why the eye area is such a hotspot

The skin around the eyes is thinner and has fewer, smaller sebaceous glands than the rest of the face, which might seem like it should make milia less likely there, but in practice this area is one of the most common sites. Part of the explanation lies in the type and amount of product regularly applied to this area. Heavy, richly occlusive eye creams — particularly those containing a lot of dense oils or waxes — can, in some people, contribute to keratin becoming trapped in this thinner, more delicate skin, especially with long-term daily use of a product that's genuinely too heavy for the individual's skin type. Sun damage over years can also thicken and stiffen the skin in this area in ways that make it more prone to trapping keratin. This is why some people notice a cluster of small milia developing gradually along the lower eyelid or under-eye area over months or years of consistent, unchanged product use, even when nothing else about their skin or routine has obviously changed.

## Evidence-based extraction and prevention

Because milia sit in a sealed pocket beneath intact skin, professional extraction remains the most reliable, immediate way to remove an existing milium. This is typically performed by a dermatologist, qualified aesthetician or skin care professional using a sterile fine needle or small lancet to create a tiny opening directly over the milium, after which the trapped keratin plug is gently expressed out with light pressure. Done correctly by a trained professional, this is quick, low-risk and generally leaves no mark, particularly on non-eye areas; the eye area specifically warrants extra caution and is best left to an experienced professional given how delicate and thin the surrounding skin is.

At-home squeezing or attempting to needle milia yourself is strongly discouraged, not because it's impossible to do safely in principle, but because the risk of infection, scarring or damaging the surrounding skin (particularly around the eyes) is meaningfully higher without proper sterile technique, adequate lighting, magnification and experience. Many people who attempt this at home report bruising, prolonged redness or simply an inability to fully express the milium because the overlying skin wasn't opened cleanly, sometimes making the area worse or more inflamed than before.

For prevention and for milder, more superficial milia, gentle chemical exfoliation can help by encouraging faster skin cell turnover, which can prevent keratin from accumulating and trapping in the first place, and may help resolve very superficial milia over time without extraction. Retinoids, including over-the-counter retinol used cautiously around the eye area and prescription-strength options elsewhere on the face, are commonly recommended for milia-prone skin because they promote skin renewal and can reduce the likelihood of new milia forming, particularly the secondary type linked to skin damage or heavy product buildup. Reviewing and potentially lightening up eye cream and rich facial moisturiser formulations, particularly for anyone noticing recurrent milia specifically around the eyes, is a sensible, low-risk first step that addresses a genuinely common contributing factor.

## Common mistakes people make

1. Attempting to squeeze or pop milia at home the way one would a pimple, which usually fails because there is no open pore to express through, and can cause bruising or damage to surrounding skin.
2. Using a pin or needle without proper sterilisation, risking infection, particularly given how close to the eye many milia form.
3. Assuming milia are a hygiene issue and over-cleansing or over-exfoliating the area, which won't resolve existing milia and can irritate delicate skin.
4. Continuing to use a very heavy, occlusive eye cream or moisturiser despite recurrent milia, without considering it as a contributing factor.
5. Confusing milia with whiteheads and treating them with standard acne spot treatments, which have little to no effect on a sealed keratin cyst.
6. Waiting for milia to "pop" on their own the way a pimple might, when in reality many persist unchanged for months or years without professional extraction.

## Key facts

- Milia form when keratin becomes trapped in a closed pocket beneath the skin, unlike acne, which forms within an open follicle.
- They are extremely common in newborns, typically resolving on their own within a few weeks of life.
- In adults, they most commonly appear around the eyes, eyelids and cheeks.
- Professional extraction using a sterile lancet or fine needle is the most reliable way to remove an established milium.
- Heavy, occlusive eye creams and long-term use of very rich facial products are recognised contributing factors in some people.
- Milia do not respond to standard acne treatments like benzoyl peroxide, since there is no bacterial or follicular component involved.

## What this means for your skin

If you have milia, resist the urge to treat them like pimples — squeezing, picking or applying acne spot treatments won't work and risks unnecessary irritation or scarring. For existing, established milia that bother you, book a professional extraction with a dermatologist or experienced aesthetician rather than attempting it yourself, particularly for anything near the eye. For prevention, take a look at your current eye cream and rich moisturisers, and consider whether a lighter-weight formulation might suit your skin better, especially if milia keep recurring in the same spots. Gentle, consistent chemical exfoliation across the rest of the face can support overall skin turnover and may reduce the frequency of new milia forming over time, though some people remain simply more prone to them regardless of routine changes.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant for anyone who has noticed small, firm, pearly-white bumps around the eyes, eyelids or cheeks that haven't responded to typical skincare or acne treatments. It's particularly relevant for people who use heavy, richly occlusive eye creams or facial moisturisers long-term, and for anyone recovering from skin trauma such as sunburn, blistering, dermabrasion or certain inflammatory skin conditions, given the recognised link with secondary milia formation. Parents of newborns should also understand that milia on a baby's nose and face are extremely common and typically resolve on their own, requiring no treatment or concern.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Gentle chemical exfoliants (glycolic, lactic or salicylic acid) at moderate strength, to support skin turnover
- Over-the-counter retinol, used cautiously and sparingly around the eye area
- Prescription-strength retinoids for more targeted skin renewal support elsewhere on the face
- Lightweight, non-comedogenic eye creams and moisturisers, particularly for anyone recurrently prone to milia
- Sterile lancets or fine needles, used only by trained professionals for extraction, never at home
- Broad-spectrum sunscreen, to reduce the skin thickening and sun damage that can contribute to secondary milia over time

## The South African context

Milia aren't especially climate-dependent, but a couple of South African-specific factors are worth noting. The country's consistently high UV index means cumulative sun damage, a recognised contributor to secondary milia via skin thickening over years, is a real long-term consideration, reinforcing the broader case for daily SPF regardless of the specific concern being managed. On the retail side, professional milia extraction is offered by dermatologists and many qualified aestheticians and skin clinics across South Africa's major centres, generally at an accessible price point as a quick, single-session procedure, and it's worth specifically asking a clinic whether the practitioner has experience extracting milia around the eye area given how delicate that skin is. Lightweight, non-comedogenic eye creams and gentle exfoliating serums are widely available through Clicks, Dis-Chem, Dermastore and Takealot, making a simple prevention-focused product swap an easy, low-cost first step before considering professional extraction for existing milia.

## Frequently asked questions

- Can I pop milia like a pimple? No, milia sit in a closed pocket beneath the skin with no natural opening, so squeezing generally doesn't work and can bruise or damage the surrounding area.
- Are milia a sign of poor skincare or hygiene? Not typically — they're related to keratin trapping beneath the skin, which can be linked to product choice or skin trauma, but isn't a hygiene issue in the way acne sometimes is perceived to be.
- Will milia go away on their own? Some superficial milia gradually resolve over weeks to months, particularly with gentle exfoliation support, but many persist unchanged until professionally extracted.
- Is milia extraction painful? Professional extraction using a sterile lancet is generally quick and only mildly uncomfortable, similar to a small pinprick, and doesn't typically require anaesthesia.

## What to do this week

1. Avoid squeezing or attempting to needle any milia at home this week.
2. Review your current eye cream and rich moisturisers for heaviness, and consider a lighter formulation if milia are recurrent.
3. Introduce or continue gentle chemical exfoliation on the rest of the face, avoiding the delicate eye area unless a product is specifically formulated for it.
4. Book a consultation with a dermatologist or experienced aesthetician if you have milia you'd like professionally extracted.
5. Confirm daily broad-spectrum sunscreen use, particularly around the eye area, to reduce long-term contributing sun damage.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
