  $skt$centella-asiatica-cica-tiger-grass$skt$,
  $skt$What Centella Asiatica (Cica) Really Does For Sensitive, Irritated Skin$skt$,
  $skt$Centella Asiatica, better known as cica or tiger grass, is a calming plant extract that speeds up skin recovery and takes the sting out of a compromised barrier.$skt$,
  $skb$Centella Asiatica, most commonly known in skincare as cica or tiger grass, is a calming plant extract with a genuine, long medicinal history behind it, and it works by supporting the skin's own repair processes rather than just masking redness temporarily.

## Quick answer

- Centella Asiatica is a herb traditionally used for wound healing, long before it became a skincare ingredient known as cica.
- Its key active compounds, madecassoside, asiaticoside, madecassic acid and asiatic acid, support collagen synthesis and calm inflammation.
- It's especially effective for red, irritated, compromised or freshly-exfoliated skin.
- Cica products range widely in texture, from watery essences to thick recovery balms, depending on how much barrier support is needed.
- It layers well with virtually every other skincare ingredient and carries very low irritation risk itself.

## Where cica comes from and what it actually contains

Centella Asiatica is a small, ground-creeping herb native to wetland regions across Asia, and it has a documented history in traditional medicine stretching back centuries, used topically to support wound healing and reduce inflammation long before any cosmetic chemist isolated its individual compounds. The name tiger grass comes from an often-repeated bit of folklore that tigers were observed rolling in the plant to heal wounds, which is more charming anecdote than verified science, but it captures why the plant earned its reputation.

What matters for skincare purposes is the plant's chemical profile. Centella Asiatica contains a group of compounds called triterpenoids, the most cited of which are asiaticoside, madecassoside, asiatic acid and madecassic acid. Each of these has been studied for slightly different effects: they're associated with supporting collagen production, calming inflammatory pathways, and encouraging the kind of tissue repair relevant to wound healing, scarring and general skin recovery. This is a genuinely different mechanism from a purely occlusive or humectant calming ingredient, because cica isn't just sitting on top of irritated skin, it's understood to actively support the underlying repair processes.

Cica became a major skincare trend through Korean beauty brands, which popularised extract-rich, high-concentration cica lines specifically marketed at calming and repairing compromised skin, and the trend then spread globally as consumers increasingly reached for gentler, barrier-supportive routines after years of aggressive multi-acid, multi-retinoid regimens left a lot of people with reactive, over-treated skin.

## How cica compares to other calming ingredients

It's useful to place cica alongside the other big names in the calming ingredient category, since they often show up together in the same barrier-repair products.

Compared to niacinamide, cica is more specifically associated with active repair and wound-healing support, while niacinamide's calming effect comes more from barrier strengthening and oil regulation. They're frequently combined rather than substituted for each other.

Compared to allantoin, a smaller, simpler soothing compound often used in sunburn and after-sun products, cica offers a broader, more complex set of active compounds with a more established traditional-medicine and repair-focused pedigree, though both share the general goal of calming irritated skin.

Compared to aloe vera, another traditional calming plant extract, cica tends to have a stronger evidence base specifically around collagen support and structural repair, while aloe is more associated with immediate cooling, hydration and mild anti-inflammatory relief, particularly for sunburn.

Compared to colloidal oatmeal, a dermatologist favourite for eczema and highly reactive skin, cica and oatmeal both calm and protect a compromised barrier, but oatmeal's mechanism leans more toward forming a physical protective film, while cica's leans more toward supporting the skin's underlying repair biology.

In practice, many of the most effective barrier-repair and redness-calming products combine several of these ingredients rather than relying on just one, since they approach the same problem from complementary angles.

## When and how to use centella asiatica

Cica products come in a genuinely wide range of formats and concentrations, which matters because the right one depends on how compromised your skin currently is.

1. For general daily calming and prevention, a lightweight cica essence, toner or serum used under your regular moisturiser works well as an ongoing addition to a routine, even for skin that isn't currently irritated.
2. For active irritation, such as a fresh sunburn, post-exfoliation redness, retinoid purging or a reaction to a new product, reach for a higher-concentration cica cream or balm, applied generously and more frequently than a typical serum, sometimes several times a day during an acute flare.
3. For post-procedure recovery, such as after a facial treatment, extractions, microneedling or laser, cica-based products are commonly recommended by practitioners specifically because of the repair-supportive compounds, applied as directed by whoever performed the treatment.
4. Cica can be layered under sunscreen and makeup without issue, and most formulations absorb well enough not to interfere with subsequent steps.
5. There's no need to cycle on and off cica the way you might with a strong active, since it's considered safe for continuous, long-term daily use.
6. If using a thick cica balm as an emergency measure, it's fine to apply it as the very last step of a routine, over moisturiser, as a kind of protective seal during a flare.

## Common mistakes and myths

The biggest mistake is assuming any product marketed as cica is equally potent. Because the trend exploded in popularity, cica now appears on products with wildly different actual concentrations of Centella Asiatica extract, from serious barrier-repair formulas to products where it's little more than a marketing name-check far down the ingredient list. If you're buying cica specifically to treat active irritation, check that the extract is listed reasonably high in the ingredient order, not buried near the preservatives.

A second mistake is expecting cica to substitute for actually addressing the cause of irritation. If your skin is compromised because you're over-exfoliating or layering too many actives, applying cica on top without also reducing that load treats the symptom without addressing the underlying cause, and irritation is likely to recur.

A third misconception is thinking cica is only for reactive or sensitive skin types. Because it's genuinely gentle and broadly compatible, it's a reasonable everyday ingredient for any skin type, including oily and acne-prone skin, particularly as a calming layer alongside acne treatments that can be drying or irritating.

Finally, some people confuse cica with unrelated meanings of the acronym, or assume it's a synthetic lab ingredient because of the trendy short name. It's worth remembering this is a genuine, whole plant extract with a long history, not a novel synthetic compound.

## Key facts

- Key active compounds: asiaticoside, madecassoside, asiatic acid and madecassic acid.
- Traditional use: wound healing and skin recovery in various Asian traditional medicine systems, long predating cosmetic use.
- Popularised globally through Korean skincare, where it remains a category staple.
- Suitable for continuous, long-term daily use with no need for cycling breaks.
- Available across a wide texture range: essences, serums, creams, sheet masks and thick recovery balms.
- Considered one of the lowest-irritation-risk ingredients in mainstream skincare, suitable even for very reactive skin.

## What this means for your skin

If your skin regularly feels reactive, easily flushed, prone to stinging with new products, or if you're recovering from sunburn, over-exfoliation, a reaction, or a professional treatment, cica is one of the more reliably gentle and genuinely functional options to reach for. Unlike some calming ingredients that primarily provide a temporary cooling or soothing sensation, cica is associated with supporting the skin's actual repair processes, which makes it a sensible long-term addition rather than only an emergency measure.

## Who should pay attention

- Sensitive, reactive or easily-flushed skin
- Anyone actively using retinoids, acids or other strong actives who wants a calming layer to offset irritation
- Post-sunburn or post-sun-exposure skin
- Post-procedure skin recovering from facials, extractions, microneedling or laser treatments
- Rosacea-prone skin, as part of a broader calming routine
- Eczema-adjacent or barrier-compromised skin, alongside ceramide-based moisturisers

There's essentially no population that needs to avoid cica; it's considered one of the safest, best-tolerated categories of skincare ingredient available.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Niacinamide: complementary barrier-support ingredient, commonly formulated together
- Ceramides: pair well for structural barrier repair alongside cica's active healing support
- Allantoin: a simpler, gentler soothing compound often combined with cica in after-sun and sensitive-skin formulas
- Aloe vera: a comparable calming plant extract, often used together for immediate cooling relief
- Panthenol, also known as provitamin B5: frequently paired with cica in barrier-repair and recovery-focused products
- Retinoids and acids: cica is commonly used specifically to buffer irritation from these actives, not to conflict with them

## The South African context

South Africa's strong UV exposure and the resulting frequency of sunburn, combined with a skincare culture that has increasingly embraced multi-step, active-heavy routines, makes cica genuinely useful locally as both a preventative daily calming layer and an after-sun or post-active recovery tool. The country's dry winter interior climate also compounds barrier stress, making cica a sensible year-round addition rather than a seasonal-only product.

Cica products, largely driven by the popularity of Korean skincare brands, are widely available in South Africa through Dis-Chem, Clicks, Takealot and Dermastore, spanning both accessible drugstore-tier options and higher-end dermo-cosmetic ranges. Given how common sun-related irritation, active-ingredient overuse and reactive skin are in the South African market, cica has become a reasonable staple recommendation rather than a niche trend product.

## Frequently asked questions

- Is cica the same as Centella Asiatica? Yes, cica is simply the popular shorthand name for Centella Asiatica in skincare marketing, most associated with Korean beauty products.
- Can I use cica every day? Yes, it's considered safe and appropriate for continuous daily use, with no need to cycle it in and out of a routine.
- Does cica help with acne scars? It's more associated with supporting general skin repair and inflammation reduction than specifically fading pigmented scars, though a calmer healing process may indirectly reduce the risk of dark marks forming.
- Can sensitive skin use cica with retinol? Yes, cica is commonly used specifically to buffer the dryness and irritation that can come with retinol, applied as a calming layer alongside or after it.

## What to do this week

1. Check whether any current sensitive-skin or after-sun product you own lists Centella Asiatica or cica extract, and where it sits in the ingredient order.
2. If your skin is currently irritated from sun, exfoliation or a new active, introduce a cica cream or balm as a calming layer for the next week.
3. Consider adding a lightweight cica essence or serum to your daily routine if you don't currently have a dedicated calming step.
4. Keep a cica balm on hand specifically for sunburn or post-treatment recovery, applying generously as needed.
5. Reduce any concurrent over-exfoliation or active stacking at the same time you introduce cica, so you're addressing the cause of irritation, not just the symptom.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
  ARRAY[$skt$Centella Asiatica, also called cica or tiger grass, is a plant extract rich in compounds that calm inflammation and support wound healing.$skt$, $skt$Its active compounds include madecassoside, asiaticoside, madecassic acid and asiatic acid, each contributing to repair and calming effects.$skt$, $skt$It's a staple of Korean skincare, originally used medicinally for wound healing long before it became a cosmetic trend.$skt$, $skt$Cica products range from lightweight essences to thick balms, suited to different levels of irritation.$skt$, $skt$It's especially useful after sunburn, over-exfoliation, retinoid irritation or during general barrier repair.$skt$]::text[],
  $skt$Centella Asiatica$skt$,
  $skt$SkinLabs Editorial Team$skt$,
  $skt$$skt$,
  $skt$2026-08-25$skt$::date,
  $skt$9 min read$skt$,
  1843,
  $skt$https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1532187863486-abf9dbad1b69?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80$skt$,
  $skt$A pipette dispensing liquid into laboratory test tubes$skt$,
  $skt$Louis Reed$skt$,
  $skt$https://unsplash.com/@_louisreed?utm_source=SkinLabs&utm_medium=referral$skt$,
  '[]'::jsonb,
  $skt$Centella Asiatica (Cica): Benefits and How to Use It$skt$,
  $skt$How centella asiatica, or cica, soothes irritation and supports skin repair, plus how to use it and where to buy it in SA.$skt$,
  $skt${"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "Article", "headline": "What Centella Asiatica (Cica) Really Does For Sensitive, Irritated Skin", "description": "How centella asiatica, or cica, soothes irritation and supports skin repair, plus how to use it and where to buy it in SA.", "image": ["https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1532187863486-abf9dbad1b69?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1600&q=80"], "datePublished": "2026-08-25", "dateModified": "2026-08-25", "mainEntityOfPage": {"@type": "WebPage", "@id": "https://skinlabs.co.za/newsroom/centella-asiatica-cica-tiger-grass"}, "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "The Daily Skinny by SkinLabs"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "SkinLabs", "logo": {"@type": "ImageObject", "url": "https://skinlabs.co.za/pwa-512.png"}}, "inLanguage": "en-ZA"}$skt$::jsonb,
