  $skt$concealing-and-treating-broken-capillaries$skt$,
  $skt$Broken Capillaries: Why They Happen and How to Conceal and Treat Them$skt$,
  $skt$Broken capillaries are permanently dilated blood vessels near the skin surface, and while makeup can hide them today, only specific treatments actually reduce them.$skt$,
  $skb$Broken capillaries are small, permanently widened blood vessels sitting close to the skin's surface, most often on the cheeks and around the nose, and while no skincare product truly reverses them, the right concealing technique and a few targeted treatments can make a real visible difference.

## Quick answer

- Broken capillaries, medically called telangiectasia, are tiny blood vessels that have become permanently dilated and visible through the skin, not vessels that have actually ruptured.
- Common triggers include chronic sun exposure, extreme temperature changes, alcohol, spicy food, certain skin conditions like rosacea, and genetics.
- Skincare can calm the surrounding redness and inflammation but cannot close or shrink the dilated vessel itself.
- Colour-correcting concealer, applied before skin-tone concealer, is the most effective at-home way to visually neutralise them.
- In-clinic vascular laser or light-based treatments are the only approaches with real evidence for meaningfully reducing their visibility long term.

## What broken capillaries actually are

The name is slightly misleading. Broken capillaries are not vessels that have physically burst or torn, in most cases; they are small blood vessels, typically capillaries or slightly larger venules, that have lost their normal elasticity and remain permanently dilated, or widened, rather than constricting back to their normal size the way healthy vessels do. Because they sit close to the surface of thin facial skin, particularly around the nose, cheeks and chin, that persistent widening shows through as fine red or purple thread-like lines, sometimes called spider veins when they branch out from a central point.

The skin in these areas, especially the cheeks and around the nose, tends to be thinner and has a particularly dense network of small blood vessels close to the surface, which is exactly why capillaries become visible here long before they would show anywhere else on the body. Once a vessel wall loses its elasticity in this way, it generally stays dilated; skincare products can reduce the surrounding inflammation and blotchiness, but there is no topical ingredient capable of physically shrinking or removing an already-dilated blood vessel.

Several distinct triggers contribute to this loss of vessel elasticity over time. Chronic sun exposure is one of the most significant, because UV radiation damages the collagen and elastic fibres that support and structure the walls of small blood vessels, in much the same way it damages collagen elsewhere in the skin. Repeated extreme temperature changes, moving quickly between hot and cold environments, hot showers, saunas, and cold winter wind, cause blood vessels to repeatedly dilate and constrict, which over years can wear down their structural resilience. Alcohol consumption causes blood vessels to dilate temporarily, and frequent, heavy use is associated with more persistent vessel visibility over time. Spicy food, intense exercise and emotional flushing all trigger the same temporary dilation, and repeated triggering can contribute to vessels eventually failing to fully constrict back down. Genetics also play a real role independent of lifestyle, which is why some people develop visible capillaries at a young age with none of the other typical triggers. Certain skin conditions, most notably rosacea, are strongly associated with persistent facial redness and visible capillaries as a core feature of the condition itself, rather than a side effect.

## Why broken capillaries get mismanaged

The most common mistake is trying to treat visible capillaries the same way you would treat pigmentation, reaching for brightening serums, acids or vitamin C in the hope of fading them the way you would a sun spot. These ingredients can genuinely help the general tone and inflammation of surrounding skin, and are worth using for overall skin health, but they have essentially no effect on an already-dilated blood vessel, because the mechanism is completely different: pigmentation is about melanin in skin cells, while a broken capillary is a structural, vascular issue.

A second common mistake is using only a standard, skin-tone-matched concealer directly over the area. Red or purple discolouration sits at the opposite end of the colour wheel from typical skin tones, so a flesh-toned concealer alone often needs to be applied so thickly to fully hide the redness that it looks cakey and unnatural, or simply fails to fully neutralise the colour underneath.

A third mistake is assuming nothing meaningful can be done and either giving up on concealing them altogether or, at the other extreme, expecting an over-the-counter product to deliver clinic-level results it is not capable of. Realistic expectations, understanding what skincare can and cannot do here, saves both money and frustration.

## Evidence-based ways to conceal and actually treat them

Concealing well and treating the underlying vessels are two separate goals, and it helps to approach them separately rather than expecting one product to do both.

1. Use a green or salmon-toned colour corrector, applied thinly and directly over the visible capillaries before your regular concealer, since green and salmon sit opposite red and purple tones on the colour wheel and neutralise them far more effectively than skin-tone concealer alone.
2. Pat, rather than rub, both the colour corrector and the concealer into place, since rubbing can drag pigment unevenly and, over the longer term, repeated friction on already fragile-vessel-prone skin is best minimised.
3. Set the area lightly with a translucent powder to help the concealer last through the day without needing to reapply and disturb the area repeatedly.
4. For the underlying vessels themselves, in-clinic treatments such as certain vascular laser and intense pulsed light therapies are the options with genuine evidence for reducing visibility, working by targeting the haemoglobin in the dilated vessel with heat, causing it to gradually collapse and be reabsorbed by the body.
5. Supportive skincare, including gentle, fragrance-free formulas, barrier-supporting ceramides, and daily sunscreen, will not remove existing capillaries but does help prevent new ones from forming as quickly and calms the general redness and irritation that often surrounds them.
6. Avoiding known personal triggers, extreme heat, very hot water on the face, excessive alcohol, when practical, reduces the ongoing dilation that can accelerate new capillary formation, particularly for anyone who also has rosacea.

Recovery and improvement timelines differ sharply between concealing and treating. Concealing is immediate, a well-applied colour-corrected look lasts the day. Treating existing vessels with in-clinic options generally requires a small series of sessions over a few months, with continued gradual fading visible for weeks after each session as the treated vessel is reabsorbed. Skincare-only approaches to the vessels themselves will not produce a comparable structural result, however consistently used, and it is worth being clear-eyed about that rather than cycling through serums expecting vascular change.

## Key facts

- The medical term for visible broken capillaries is telangiectasia, referring to permanently dilated small blood vessels near the skin's surface.
- The cheeks and nose have thinner skin and a denser surface capillary network than most of the rest of the face, which is why capillaries show up there first.
- UV damage to the collagen and elastic fibres supporting vessel walls is one of the primary long-term contributors to vessel dilation.
- Topical skincare ingredients cannot shrink or close an already-dilated blood vessel; only heat-based vascular treatments have real evidence for that.
- Rosacea is strongly associated with persistent visible facial capillaries and should be considered as a possible underlying cause if redness is widespread and persistent rather than isolated.

## What this means for your skin

If your main concern is how capillaries look day to day, invest in a proper colour-correcting technique rather than more concealer alone. If you want to meaningfully reduce their visibility long term, understand that this generally means an in-clinic, vascular-targeted treatment rather than a skincare product, and set expectations and budget accordingly. Either way, daily sunscreen and gentle temperature habits help slow the formation of new capillaries even if they cannot undo existing ones.

## Who should pay attention

This is most relevant to people with naturally fair or thin skin, anyone with a family history of visible facial vessels, people who spend significant time in the sun without daily protection, anyone with diagnosed or suspected rosacea, and people who have noticed new fine red or purple lines developing on the cheeks or around the nose over the past few years. It is also worth attention for anyone who drinks alcohol regularly or works in environments with frequent hot-to-cold temperature swings, such as commercial kitchens.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Green-toned colour corrector, for neutralising red-toned capillaries before concealer
- Salmon or peach-toned colour corrector, an alternative that works well on a wider range of underlying skin tones
- Fragrance-free, barrier-supporting moisturisers containing ceramides, to reduce general irritation around fragile areas
- Niacinamide, for its general anti-inflammatory and barrier-supporting benefits, though not for the vessels themselves
- Broad-spectrum sunscreen, to slow further UV-driven vessel wall damage
- Gentle, non-abrasive cleansers, avoiding hot water and harsh scrubbing over affected areas

## The South African context

South Africa's high year-round UV index makes chronic sun-driven vessel damage a genuine local concern, particularly for fair-skinned South Africans and anyone who spends significant time outdoors without daily sunscreen. Rapid indoor-outdoor temperature swings are common locally too, especially the contrast between heated or air-conditioned indoor spaces and outdoor heat or Highveld winter cold, which can be a contributing trigger for some people. Colour-correcting concealers and vascular-supportive skincare are both readily available at Clicks, Dis-Chem and Dermastore, and in-clinic vascular treatments are offered by dermatologists and reputable aesthetic clinics in most major South African cities, so both the concealing and the treating route are locally accessible.

## Frequently asked questions

- Will using vitamin C or retinol fade broken capillaries? These ingredients support overall skin health and mild redness but do not shrink an already-dilated blood vessel; they are not a substitute for vascular-specific treatment.
- Is a green concealer really necessary, or will any concealer do? A colour corrector in a shade opposite red on the colour wheel, such as green or salmon, neutralises redness far more effectively and with less product than skin-tone concealer alone.
- Can broken capillaries come back after treatment? New capillaries can form over time from ongoing triggers like sun exposure, so continued sun protection and trigger management help maintain results after in-clinic treatment.
- Are broken capillaries a sign of an underlying condition? Sometimes, particularly if accompanied by persistent flushing, sensitivity or bumps, which can indicate rosacea and is worth discussing with a dermatologist for proper diagnosis.

## What to do this week

1. Try a green or salmon colour corrector under your regular concealer and note the difference in coverage compared with concealer alone.
2. Switch to lukewarm water for face washing if you have been using hot water, particularly if capillaries are on the nose or cheeks.
3. Add daily sunscreen if you are not already using it consistently, focusing on full facial coverage.
4. Note any personal triggers, alcohol, spicy food, extreme heat, that seem to make redness worse, and moderate them where practical.
5. If capillaries are widespread, persistent or accompanied by flushing and sensitivity, book a consultation with a dermatologist to discuss in-clinic options and rule out rosacea.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
  ARRAY[$skt$Broken capillaries are small, permanently dilated blood vessels visible through the skin, not actual breaks.$skt$, $skt$Sun exposure, heat, alcohol, extreme temperature changes and genetics are the main triggers.$skt$, $skt$Colour-correcting concealer, not just skin-tone concealer, is what actually hides redness effectively.$skt$, $skt$In-clinic vascular treatments are the only reliable way to meaningfully reduce existing capillaries.$skt$]::text[],
