  $skt$body-care-acids-for-body$skt$,
  $skt$The Body Acid Routine Most People Are Missing$skt$,
  $skt$How to use AHAs and BHAs on body skin to fix rough texture, keratosis pilaris and back or chest breakouts.$skt$,
  $skb$Body skin can generally tolerate stronger and more frequent exfoliating acids than facial skin, which is exactly why a proper body acid routine, built around AHAs and BHAs, is one of the most underused tools for smoother, clearer, more even-toned skin from the neck down.

## Quick answer

- Body skin is thicker and more resilient than facial skin, meaning it can generally handle higher concentrations of AHAs and BHAs, and more frequent use, than the face.
- Glycolic and lactic acid lotions address rough texture, dullness and keratosis pilaris, the small rough bumps often found on the upper arms and thighs.
- Salicylic acid, a BHA, is particularly effective for body acne on the back, chest and shoulders, since it can penetrate oil and get into congested pores.
- Introduce body acids gradually, the same rule as facial actives, starting a few nights a week and increasing based on tolerance.
- Sunscreen still matters on exposed body areas, especially if you are using acids there, since exfoliated skin is more sensitive to UV exposure.

## Building a body acid routine, step by step

Body skincare has historically lagged behind facial skincare in sophistication, with many people still relying on a basic moisturiser alone, if that. Given how much surface area body skin covers, and how common issues like keratosis pilaris, body acne and rough, dull texture are, a proper acid-based routine can make a genuinely significant difference.

1. Start in the shower with a gentle, non-stripping body wash rather than a harsh, heavily fragranced soap, since body skin, like facial skin, has a barrier worth protecting even though it is generally more resilient.
2. Pat skin mostly dry, leaving it slightly damp, then apply a body lotion or serum containing glycolic or lactic acid to areas prone to rough texture, commonly the upper arms, thighs and lower legs.
3. For body acne on the back, chest or shoulders, apply a salicylic acid body wash or leave-on lotion specifically to those areas, since BHAs are oil-soluble and particularly effective at getting into clogged, oily pores.
4. Start with two to three applications a week for any new body acid product, regardless of area, and increase gradually over several weeks as tolerance builds, exactly as you would with a facial active.
5. Follow with a plain, fragrance-conscious body moisturiser over the rest of the body, and layer it over the treated areas too once the acid product has absorbed, since even resilient body skin benefits from consistent hydration alongside exfoliation.
6. Apply sunscreen to any exposed body areas being treated with acids, arms, legs, chest, particularly relevant in South Africa's high-UV climate, since acid-treated skin is more vulnerable to sun-related irritation and pigmentation.
7. Reassess after six to eight weeks. Rough texture, keratosis pilaris bumps and body acne all typically show meaningful improvement in this timeframe with consistent use, though keratosis pilaris in particular often requires ongoing maintenance rather than a one-time fix.

## Why body skin can handle more than facial skin

Body skin, particularly on the arms, legs and torso, is generally thicker than facial skin and has a slightly different lipid composition and turnover rate, which is part of why body acid products are often formulated at higher concentrations than their facial equivalents, and why they are frequently well tolerated at a higher frequency of use. This is also why keratosis pilaris, a very common condition where dead skin cells accumulate around hair follicles causing small, rough bumps often on the upper arms, responds so well to consistent AHA or BHA use, since these ingredients directly address the excess keratin buildup that causes the bumps.

Body acne, most common on the back, chest and shoulders, shares similar underlying mechanisms to facial acne, excess oil production, clogged pores and inflammation, but often responds particularly well to salicylic acid specifically because of its oil solubility, allowing it to penetrate into the sebum-filled pores common in these acne-prone body areas. This is why a dedicated salicylic acid body wash or leave-on treatment is frequently more effective for body acne than simply using a facial acne product on the body, or vice versa.

## Common mistakes with body acids

- Assuming body skin needs no exfoliation at all, and using nothing beyond a basic moisturiser, missing an opportunity to address common issues like keratosis pilaris and rough texture.
- Applying a strong facial-strength retinoid or a very high-percentage acid to the body all at once without any gradual introduction, since even resilient body skin can become irritated if overloaded too quickly.
- Using a body scrub with rough physical exfoliating granules instead of, or alongside, a chemical exfoliant, which can cause micro-tears and irritation, particularly on already sensitised or acne-prone skin.
- Skipping sunscreen on treated body areas, especially the arms, chest and lower legs, all commonly exposed and all vulnerable to increased sun sensitivity while using exfoliating acids.
- Expecting keratosis pilaris to resolve permanently after a few weeks, when it typically requires ongoing, consistent use to keep the bumps controlled, since it tends to return once treatment stops.
- Layering too many new body products at once, a new acid lotion, a new body wash, and a new moisturiser simultaneously, making it hard to identify the source if irritation occurs.

## Adapting body acid use to your specific concerns

- For keratosis pilaris specifically, a lactic acid or glycolic acid lotion in the ten to fifteen percent range, used consistently three to five times a week, tends to produce the most noticeable improvement in bump texture and redness over several weeks.
- For body acne on the back, chest or shoulders, a salicylic acid body wash left on the skin for a minute or two before rinsing, combined with a leave-on salicylic acid lotion on particularly stubborn areas, is a commonly recommended combination.
- For general dullness or rough texture without a specific diagnosed condition, a lower-strength, gentler glycolic or lactic acid body lotion used two to three times a week is a reasonable, low-risk starting point.
- For sensitive skin or areas prone to irritation, such as inner thighs or underarms, start with a lower concentration and less frequent use than you would on more resilient areas like the outer arms or shins.
- For very dry, flaky skin on the lower legs, common in colder months or drier climates, combine a gentle AHA with a genuinely rich moisturiser, since exfoliation alone without adequate hydration can leave skin feeling tight and uncomfortable.

## Key facts

- Body skin is generally thicker than facial skin, allowing many people to tolerate higher acid concentrations and more frequent use on the body than on the face.
- Keratosis pilaris is caused by a buildup of keratin around hair follicles and is a very common, harmless condition that responds well to consistent AHA or BHA use.
- Salicylic acid's oil solubility makes it particularly effective for acne on oilier, more sebum-heavy body areas like the back and chest.
- Improvement in body texture, keratosis pilaris and body acne typically becomes noticeable after six to eight weeks of consistent use, similar to facial active ingredient timelines.
- Exfoliated, acid-treated skin is more sensitive to UV exposure, making sunscreen relevant on any exposed, treated body area.

## What this means for your skin

A body acid routine addresses a genuine gap in most people's skincare, since the face typically receives far more attention and product sophistication than the rest of the body despite the body covering vastly more surface area. Consistent use of the right acid for the right concern, glycolic or lactic for texture and keratosis pilaris, salicylic for body acne, produces real, visible improvement over a matter of weeks, and ongoing maintenance keeps results stable over time. This is a routine that rewards patience and consistency in exactly the same way facial actives do, rather than being a quick fix.

## Who should pay attention

This applies to anyone dealing with rough, bumpy texture on the upper arms or thighs, commonly keratosis pilaris, anyone dealing with recurring breakouts on the back, chest or shoulders, and anyone whose body skin generally feels dull, rough or uneven compared with a well-maintained facial skincare routine. It is also relevant for anyone who has previously only used a basic moisturiser on their body and is curious about a more targeted approach, and for people who exercise frequently and experience sweat-related body breakouts, since consistent salicylic acid use can meaningfully help manage this.

## Ingredients and products to know

- Glycolic acid body lotion for general texture, dullness and keratosis pilaris
- Lactic acid body lotion, a gentler alternative to glycolic acid, suited to more sensitive skin
- Salicylic acid body wash and leave-on lotion for back, chest and shoulder acne
- Urea-containing body creams, useful alongside acids for very rough or dry patches
- Fragrance-conscious, non-stripping body wash as the base of the routine
- Broad-spectrum SPF for exposed body areas, particularly the chest, arms and lower legs
- Rich, plain body moisturiser to layer over treated areas once acids have absorbed

## The South African context

South Africa's climate genuinely suits a body acid routine, warmer months and higher humidity in cities like Durban tend to increase sweating and body breakouts, making salicylic acid particularly relevant, while drier Highveld winters often worsen rough texture and keratosis pilaris, making glycolic and lactic acid lotions more useful in that season. The country's strong swimming, beach and outdoor sport culture also means a lot of skin is regularly on show and exposed to sun, both a reason to address body texture and acne, and a reason sunscreen on exposed, acid-treated areas matters more here than in less sun-intense climates. Dis-Chem, Clicks and Dermastore all carry a growing range of dedicated body acid products, keratosis pilaris-specific lotions and body acne washes, reflecting increasing local demand for more sophisticated body care beyond a basic lotion. Hard water in parts of the country can also contribute to a feeling of dryness after showering, worth factoring into body wash choice alongside any new acid product.

## Frequently asked questions

- Can I use my facial exfoliating acid on my body instead of buying a separate product? You can in a pinch, but dedicated body formulas are usually higher strength and better suited to thicker body skin, so a specific body product generally performs better.
- How long does it take to see improvement in keratosis pilaris bumps? Most people notice visible improvement in texture and redness after six to eight weeks of consistent use, though ongoing maintenance is usually needed to keep bumps controlled.
- Is it safe to use body acids every day? Many people can tolerate daily use of a well-formulated body acid product once tolerance is built up, but starting at two to three times a week and increasing gradually is the safer approach.
- Will body acids help with acne scarring on my back or chest? Consistent use can improve overall texture and tone evenness over time, but deeper scarring often benefits from additional in-clinic treatments discussed with a dermatologist.

## What to do this week

1. Identify your main body skin concern, rough texture, keratosis pilaris, or back and chest acne, and choose one targeted acid product accordingly.
2. Introduce it two to three times a week on damp, clean skin, following with a proper moisturiser.
3. Add sunscreen to any exposed body areas you are treating, particularly arms, chest and lower legs.
4. Set a reminder to reassess progress after six to eight weeks rather than judging results too early.
5. Avoid introducing more than one new body product at a time so you can clearly track what is working.

Editorial disclaimer: SkinLabs®'s views and opinions are independent. This article is educational content grounded in established dermatological consensus and is not a substitute for professional medical advice — speak to a dermatologist about persistent, severe or diagnostic concerns.$skb$,
